-- Nephron Academy — batch 2 topics, sourced from Harriet Lane Handbook 23rd ed.,
-- Ch. 19 "Nephrology" (topics 1-7) and Ch. 1 "Emergency Management" section
-- III.E Hypertensive Crisis (topic 8). Board-exam-relevant content not yet
-- covered by the existing CKD-MBD / ADPKD / NDI topics.
--
-- Each block is idempotent: it inserts a new topic if none matching the name
-- pattern exists for this owner, or updates the existing one in place
-- (without touching parent_topic_id, so any manual re-organization you've
-- done in the Academy UI is preserved).

-- ============================================================
-- 1) Renal Function Tests & Tubular Function
-- ============================================================
do $$
declare
  v_owner_id uuid;
  v_parent_id uuid;
  v_topic_id uuid;
begin
  select id into v_owner_id from auth.users where email = 'alihessamis@gmail.com' limit 1;
  if v_owner_id is null then
    raise exception 'owner not found for email alihessamis@gmail.com';
  end if;

  select id into v_parent_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and category ilike '%Renal Function Tests%' and parent_topic_id is null
    limit 1;

  select id into v_topic_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%Renal Function Tests%'
    limit 1;

  if v_topic_id is null then
    insert into public.academy_topics (
      owner_id, category, parent_topic_id, name, summary, key_points, presentation, reasoning, tests,
      interpretation, imaging, treatment, red_flags, pearls, case_stem, case_questions, case_discussion
    ) values (
      v_owner_id, 'Renal Function Tests', v_parent_id, 'Renal Function Tests & Tubular Function',
      $sum1$GFR estimation and urine-based indices are how you turn a creatinine and a few urine electrolytes into an actual clinical answer: how much filtration is left, and whether an AKI is prerenal, intrinsic, or a tubular leak. These are the calculations examiners love because they force you to show your reasoning rather than pattern-match a diagnosis.$sum1$,
      $kp1$["GFR is low at birth (~20 mL/min/1.73m^2 at term) and rises to adult values by about 2 years as renal blood flow and tubular maturation catch up — a \"normal\" creatinine in a neonate can still represent significant renal impairment.","Bedside/CKiD Schwartz eGFR = 0.413 x height(cm) / Cr(mg/dL); the classic Schwartz formula instead uses a population-specific k: 0.33 (low-birth-weight infant, first year), 0.45 (term AGA infant, first year), 0.55 (children and adolescent girls), 0.70 (adolescent boys).","FeNa = (Una/Pna)/(Ucr/Pcr) x 100: <1% suggests prerenal azotemia, >2% (often >3%) suggests intrinsic AKI/ATN; infants normally run higher (<3% still counts as prerenal) because of physiologic tubular immaturity.","FeUrea is preferred over FeNa when the patient is already on diuretics, which artificially raise urinary sodium: <35% suggests prerenal, >50% suggests intrinsic.","FeHCO3 differentiates RTA subtypes during a bicarbonate-loading test: <5% is normal or seen in distal (type 1) RTA (bicarbonate is reabsorbed normally); >15% indicates proximal (type 2) RTA (a genuine bicarbonate leak).","Urine anion gap (UNa+UK-UCl) is an indirect readout of urinary ammonium: a positive value (roughly >20) means the kidney is NOT excreting enough NH4+ despite acidosis (distal RTA); a negative value (roughly <-20) means it IS excreting NH4+ appropriately, pointing to an extrarenal bicarbonate loss such as diarrhea.","Tubular reabsorption of phosphate (TRP) = 1 - [(Up/Pp)/(Ucr/Pcr)] x 100; a low TRP with inappropriate phosphaturia suggests a tubular phosphate leak (Fanconi syndrome, XLH) rather than dietary phosphate restriction.","Spot urine calcium/creatinine ratio has an age-dependent upper limit of normal: <7 months 0.86, 7-18 months 0.60, 19 months-6 years 0.42, >6 years/adult 0.22 (mg/mg) — using the adult cutoff in an infant will over-call hypercalciuria."]$kp1$::jsonb,
      $pres1$There's no \"presentation\" for a lab test, but the clinical trigger is almost always the same: an elevated creatinine/BUN (is it prerenal or intrinsic?), a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis (which RTA?), unexplained hypophosphatemia with phosphaturia (tubular leak?), or a child with a stone/nephrocalcinosis (hypercalciuric?).$pres1$,
      $reas1$Pick the test that isolates the physiology you're questioning. Volume status and diuretic use are the two things that most often mislead FeNa/FeUrea — always check the medication list before trusting a value in the \"indeterminate\" 1-2% (FeNa) or 35-50% (FeUrea) zone. For RTA, the FeHCO3 bicarbonate-loading test and the urine anion gap are complementary, not redundant: FeHCO3 tests proximal reabsorption, UAG tests distal acidification.$reas1$,
      $tests1$Core formulas: bedside eGFR (0.413 x height/Cr), classic Schwartz eGFR (k x height/Cr), FeNa, FeUrea, FeHCO3, urine anion gap, TRP, and the spot urine Ca/Cr ratio — all implemented as calculators in this app's Labs tab (auto-computed inline whenever the matching same-day urine and plasma values are both entered).$tests1$,
      $interp1$FeNa <1% / FeUrea <35% -> prerenal; FeNa >2% / FeUrea >50% -> intrinsic (ATN); FeHCO3 <5% -> normal or distal RTA, >15% -> proximal RTA; UAG positive -> impaired distal acidification (distal or type 4 RTA), UAG negative -> extrarenal loss; Ca/Cr ratio above the age-adjusted cutoff -> hypercalciuria (screen for a stone/nephrocalcinosis workup).$interp1$,
      $img1$Not primarily imaging-based. A renal ultrasound is still the first study whenever these numbers can't distinguish prerenal/intrinsic AKI from an obstructive (postrenal) cause — obstruction has to be excluded clinically/sonographically before the fractional-excretion tests are even meaningful.$img1$,
      $tx1$These are diagnostic, not therapeutic, tools — but the result should change management directly: a prerenal pattern prompts a fluid challenge, an intrinsic pattern prompts holding nephrotoxins and treating the underlying cause, and an RTA pattern determines whether you dose alkali at a modest (distal, ~1-3 mEq/kg/day) or large (proximal, ~5-15 mEq/kg/day, often with a thiazide to reduce bicarbonate wasting) rate.$tx1$,
      $rf1$Any fractional-excretion index is invalidated by diuretics (raises FeNa falsely), and FeNa is unreliable in neonates and in early/pre-oliguric AKI. Don't compute the Ca/Cr ratio without checking the age-adjusted cutoff — a normal-for-adults ratio can be markedly abnormal in an infant.$rf1$,
      $pearl1$Board pearl: "on diuretics" in a vignette is your cue to reach for FeUrea instead of FeNa. "Positive urine anion gap with a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis" is the classic setup for distal RTA; "negative UAG" with the same acidosis points you toward diarrhea instead.$pearl1$,
      $stem1$A 3-year-old with poor growth is found to have a venous pH of 7.28, HCO3 16 mEq/L, and a normal serum anion gap. Urine studies: Na 40, K 25, Cl 30 mEq/L; urine pH 6.5.$stem1$,
      $cq1$["Calculate the urine anion gap. What does it suggest?","Given the alkaline urine pH despite systemic acidosis, what is the most likely diagnosis?","What additional metabolic findings would support a diagnosis of Fanconi syndrome instead of isolated distal RTA?"]$cq1$::jsonb,
      $disc1$UAG = 40 + 25 - 30 = +35 (positive), meaning urinary ammonium excretion is inappropriately low for the degree of acidosis — the kidney cannot acidify the urine. Combined with a urine pH persistently >5.5 despite systemic acidosis, this is classic for distal (type 1) RTA. If glucosuria, aminoaciduria, and phosphaturia (with a low TRP) were also present, that would instead suggest proximal (type 2) RTA / Fanconi syndrome, which needs a much larger alkali dose because of ongoing urinary bicarbonate wasting.$disc1$
    );
  else
    update public.academy_topics set
      category = 'Renal Function Tests',
      summary = $sum1$GFR estimation and urine-based indices are how you turn a creatinine and a few urine electrolytes into an actual clinical answer: how much filtration is left, and whether an AKI is prerenal, intrinsic, or a tubular leak. These are the calculations examiners love because they force you to show your reasoning rather than pattern-match a diagnosis.$sum1$,
      key_points = $kp1$["GFR is low at birth (~20 mL/min/1.73m^2 at term) and rises to adult values by about 2 years as renal blood flow and tubular maturation catch up — a \"normal\" creatinine in a neonate can still represent significant renal impairment.","Bedside/CKiD Schwartz eGFR = 0.413 x height(cm) / Cr(mg/dL); the classic Schwartz formula instead uses a population-specific k: 0.33 (low-birth-weight infant, first year), 0.45 (term AGA infant, first year), 0.55 (children and adolescent girls), 0.70 (adolescent boys).","FeNa = (Una/Pna)/(Ucr/Pcr) x 100: <1% suggests prerenal azotemia, >2% (often >3%) suggests intrinsic AKI/ATN; infants normally run higher (<3% still counts as prerenal) because of physiologic tubular immaturity.","FeUrea is preferred over FeNa when the patient is already on diuretics, which artificially raise urinary sodium: <35% suggests prerenal, >50% suggests intrinsic.","FeHCO3 differentiates RTA subtypes during a bicarbonate-loading test: <5% is normal or seen in distal (type 1) RTA (bicarbonate is reabsorbed normally); >15% indicates proximal (type 2) RTA (a genuine bicarbonate leak).","Urine anion gap (UNa+UK-UCl) is an indirect readout of urinary ammonium: a positive value (roughly >20) means the kidney is NOT excreting enough NH4+ despite acidosis (distal RTA); a negative value (roughly <-20) means it IS excreting NH4+ appropriately, pointing to an extrarenal bicarbonate loss such as diarrhea.","Tubular reabsorption of phosphate (TRP) = 1 - [(Up/Pp)/(Ucr/Pcr)] x 100; a low TRP with inappropriate phosphaturia suggests a tubular phosphate leak (Fanconi syndrome, XLH) rather than dietary phosphate restriction.","Spot urine calcium/creatinine ratio has an age-dependent upper limit of normal: <7 months 0.86, 7-18 months 0.60, 19 months-6 years 0.42, >6 years/adult 0.22 (mg/mg) — using the adult cutoff in an infant will over-call hypercalciuria."]$kp1$::jsonb,
      presentation = $pres1$There's no \"presentation\" for a lab test, but the clinical trigger is almost always the same: an elevated creatinine/BUN (is it prerenal or intrinsic?), a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis (which RTA?), unexplained hypophosphatemia with phosphaturia (tubular leak?), or a child with a stone/nephrocalcinosis (hypercalciuric?).$pres1$,
      reasoning = $reas1$Pick the test that isolates the physiology you're questioning. Volume status and diuretic use are the two things that most often mislead FeNa/FeUrea — always check the medication list before trusting a value in the \"indeterminate\" 1-2% (FeNa) or 35-50% (FeUrea) zone. For RTA, the FeHCO3 bicarbonate-loading test and the urine anion gap are complementary, not redundant: FeHCO3 tests proximal reabsorption, UAG tests distal acidification.$reas1$,
      tests = $tests1$Core formulas: bedside eGFR (0.413 x height/Cr), classic Schwartz eGFR (k x height/Cr), FeNa, FeUrea, FeHCO3, urine anion gap, TRP, and the spot urine Ca/Cr ratio — all implemented as calculators in this app's Labs tab (auto-computed inline whenever the matching same-day urine and plasma values are both entered).$tests1$,
      interpretation = $interp1$FeNa <1% / FeUrea <35% -> prerenal; FeNa >2% / FeUrea >50% -> intrinsic (ATN); FeHCO3 <5% -> normal or distal RTA, >15% -> proximal RTA; UAG positive -> impaired distal acidification (distal or type 4 RTA), UAG negative -> extrarenal loss; Ca/Cr ratio above the age-adjusted cutoff -> hypercalciuria (screen for a stone/nephrocalcinosis workup).$interp1$,
      imaging = $img1$Not primarily imaging-based. A renal ultrasound is still the first study whenever these numbers can't distinguish prerenal/intrinsic AKI from an obstructive (postrenal) cause — obstruction has to be excluded clinically/sonographically before the fractional-excretion tests are even meaningful.$img1$,
      treatment = $tx1$These are diagnostic, not therapeutic, tools — but the result should change management directly: a prerenal pattern prompts a fluid challenge, an intrinsic pattern prompts holding nephrotoxins and treating the underlying cause, and an RTA pattern determines whether you dose alkali at a modest (distal, ~1-3 mEq/kg/day) or large (proximal, ~5-15 mEq/kg/day, often with a thiazide to reduce bicarbonate wasting) rate.$tx1$,
      red_flags = $rf1$Any fractional-excretion index is invalidated by diuretics (raises FeNa falsely), and FeNa is unreliable in neonates and in early/pre-oliguric AKI. Don't compute the Ca/Cr ratio without checking the age-adjusted cutoff — a normal-for-adults ratio can be markedly abnormal in an infant.$rf1$,
      pearls = $pearl1$Board pearl: "on diuretics" in a vignette is your cue to reach for FeUrea instead of FeNa. "Positive urine anion gap with a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis" is the classic setup for distal RTA; "negative UAG" with the same acidosis points you toward diarrhea instead.$pearl1$,
      case_stem = $stem1$A 3-year-old with poor growth is found to have a venous pH of 7.28, HCO3 16 mEq/L, and a normal serum anion gap. Urine studies: Na 40, K 25, Cl 30 mEq/L; urine pH 6.5.$stem1$,
      case_questions = $cq1$["Calculate the urine anion gap. What does it suggest?","Given the alkaline urine pH despite systemic acidosis, what is the most likely diagnosis?","What additional metabolic findings would support a diagnosis of Fanconi syndrome instead of isolated distal RTA?"]$cq1$::jsonb,
      case_discussion = $disc1$UAG = 40 + 25 - 30 = +35 (positive), meaning urinary ammonium excretion is inappropriately low for the degree of acidosis — the kidney cannot acidify the urine. Combined with a urine pH persistently >5.5 despite systemic acidosis, this is classic for distal (type 1) RTA. If glucosuria, aminoaciduria, and phosphaturia (with a low TRP) were also present, that would instead suggest proximal (type 2) RTA / Fanconi syndrome, which needs a much larger alkali dose because of ongoing urinary bicarbonate wasting.$disc1$
    where id = v_topic_id;
  end if;
end $$;

-- ============================================================
-- 2) Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA)
-- ============================================================
do $$
declare
  v_owner_id uuid;
  v_parent_id uuid;
  v_topic_id uuid;
begin
  select id into v_owner_id from auth.users where email = 'alihessamis@gmail.com' limit 1;
  if v_owner_id is null then
    raise exception 'owner not found for email alihessamis@gmail.com';
  end if;

  select id into v_parent_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and category ilike '%Tubulopath%' and parent_topic_id is null
    limit 1;

  select id into v_topic_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%Renal Tubular Acidosis%'
    limit 1;

  if v_topic_id is null then
    insert into public.academy_topics (
      owner_id, category, parent_topic_id, name, summary, key_points, presentation, reasoning, tests,
      interpretation, imaging, treatment, red_flags, pearls, case_stem, case_questions, case_discussion
    ) values (
      v_owner_id, 'Tubulopathies', v_parent_id, 'Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA)',
      $sum2$RTA is a normal-anion-gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis caused by a tubular defect in acid-base handling despite a normal or near-normal GFR. The three pediatric-relevant types — distal (1), proximal (2), and hyperkalemic (4) — are distinguished by plasma potassium, urine pH, FeHCO3, and the urine anion gap, and each has a different treatment intensity.$sum2$,
      $kp2$["All three RTA types share a normal serum anion gap with a low serum bicarbonate — if the anion gap is elevated, it isn't RTA.","Type 1 (distal): defect in distal H+ secretion. Urine pH stays >5.5 despite systemic acidosis; plasma K is LOW; FeHCO3 is low (<5%); urine anion gap is POSITIVE; nephrocalcinosis and hypercalciuria are common (alkaline urine promotes calcium phosphate stone formation); treatment is a modest alkali dose (~1-3 mEq/kg/day).","Type 2 (proximal): defect in proximal HCO3 reabsorption. Urine CAN acidify appropriately once serum HCO3 falls low enough (so resting urine pH may be <5.5), but during a bicarbonate-loading test FeHCO3 rises above 15%; plasma K is LOW; nephrocalcinosis is rare; often part of a broader Fanconi syndrome (glucosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia); needs a MUCH larger alkali dose (~5-15 mEq/kg/day) because most of what you give is wasted in the urine, often combined with a thiazide.","Type 4 (hyperkalemic): due to aldosterone deficiency or resistance (or obstructive uropathy/PUV). Plasma K is HIGH (the hallmark that separates it from types 1 and 2); urine anion gap is positive; urine pH is variable, often <5.5; treatment targets the hyperkalemia (dietary K restriction, loop diuretic, fludrocortisone if aldosterone-deficient) — correcting the potassium often corrects the acidosis.","Fanconi syndrome is a generalized proximal tubulopathy: bicarbonaturia (type 2 RTA), glucosuria (with normal blood glucose), aminoaciduria, phosphaturia (with hypophosphatemic rickets), and low-molecular-weight proteinuria — think of it as \"type 2 RTA plus everything else the proximal tubule normally reclaims\".","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus can coexist with or mimic tubular acid-base problems but is a separate water-handling defect (covered in its own Academy topic)."]$kp2$::jsonb,
      $pres2$Failure to thrive/poor growth is the most common pediatric presentation of any chronic RTA (chronic acidosis impairs growth directly and via bone buffering). Distal RTA classically presents with a kidney stone, nephrocalcinosis on incidental ultrasound, or hypokalemic weakness. Type 4 RTA is often found incidentally on electrolytes in a neonate with obstructive uropathy, or in an older child with an unexplained hyperkalemic, mildly acidotic picture.$pres2$,
      $reas2$Step 1: confirm a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Step 2: check plasma potassium — high points to type 4, low points to type 1 or 2. Step 3: separate type 1 from type 2 using urine pH (persistently >5.5 in type 1) and, if needed, a formal FeHCO3 bicarbonate-loading test plus a look for other proximal-tubule losses (glucosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia) that would suggest Fanconi/type 2.$reas2$,
      $tests2$Venous/arterial blood gas, serum electrolytes (anion gap, K), urine pH, urine anion gap, FeHCO3 (formal test requires a sodium-bicarbonate load), urinalysis for glucosuria, and a renal ultrasound to look for nephrocalcinosis (type 1). Genetic testing is available for the inherited forms (e.g. ATP6V0A4, SLC4A1 for distal; SLC4A4 for isolated proximal).$tests2$,
      $interp2$Low K + urine pH persistently >5.5 + positive UAG -> distal (type 1). Low K + FeHCO3 >15% during bicarbonate loading +/- other Fanconi features -> proximal (type 2). High K + positive UAG -> type 4 (hypoaldosteronism/obstruction). A negative UAG with a normal-anion-gap acidosis is NOT RTA — look for an extrarenal cause (diarrhea).$interp2$,
      $img2$Renal ultrasound in suspected distal (type 1) RTA to look for nephrocalcinosis/nephrolithiasis, which is common and can itself cause obstructive complications; not routinely needed for type 2 or type 4 unless an underlying obstructive cause is suspected for type 4.$img2$,
      $tx2$Type 1: sodium bicarbonate or citrate, ~1-3 mEq/kg/day, titrated to a normal serum HCO3 — this alone typically halts further nephrocalcinosis and improves growth. Type 2: alkali at a much higher dose (~5-15 mEq/kg/day) since urinary losses continue, often plus a thiazide diuretic (mild volume contraction increases proximal HCO3 reabsorption) and potassium supplementation (alkali therapy can worsen hypokalemia). Type 4: treat the hyperkalemia first — dietary potassium restriction, loop diuretic, fludrocortisone if hypoaldosteronism is confirmed, and relieve any underlying obstruction; the acidosis usually improves once potassium normalizes.$tx2$,
      $rf2$Don't miss type 4 RTA in a newborn with PUV/obstructive uropathy who looks like they have "poor feeding and vomiting" — the hyperkalemia can be dangerous and is frequently under-recognized as tubular rather than purely due to reduced GFR. In distal RTA, undertreated chronic acidosis silently worsens both linear growth and bone mineralization even when the child looks otherwise well.$rf2$,
      $pearl2$Board pearl: potassium direction is the fastest type-4-vs-others discriminator (high K = type 4; low K = type 1 or 2). "Nephrocalcinosis + hypokalemia + alkaline urine despite acidosis" = distal (type 1) RTA. "Glucosuria with a normal blood glucose" is a giveaway for Fanconi syndrome/proximal (type 2) RTA.$pearl2$,
      $stem2$A 6-month-old boy with posterior urethral valves (status post vesicostomy) has labs: Na 138, K 6.2, Cl 112, HCO3 16 mEq/L, Cr 0.6 mg/dL (above the expected infant baseline). Urine anion gap is positive.$stem2$,
      $cq2$["What type of RTA does this picture suggest, and why?","Why is potassium the key discriminator here rather than urine pH?","What is the first step in management?"]$cq2$::jsonb,
      $disc2$This is type 4 (hyperkalemic) RTA secondary to obstructive uropathy (PUV) — the elevated potassium is the hallmark that distinguishes it from type 1 or type 2 RTA, both of which cause hypokalemia. Relieving/confirming adequate drainage of the obstruction and correcting the hyperkalemia (dietary restriction, loop diuretic +/- fludrocortisone if aldosterone response is blunted) typically improves the acidosis without needing aggressive alkali therapy alone.$disc2$
    );
  else
    update public.academy_topics set
      category = 'Tubulopathies',
      summary = $sum2$RTA is a normal-anion-gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis caused by a tubular defect in acid-base handling despite a normal or near-normal GFR. The three pediatric-relevant types — distal (1), proximal (2), and hyperkalemic (4) — are distinguished by plasma potassium, urine pH, FeHCO3, and the urine anion gap, and each has a different treatment intensity.$sum2$,
      key_points = $kp2$["All three RTA types share a normal serum anion gap with a low serum bicarbonate — if the anion gap is elevated, it isn't RTA.","Type 1 (distal): defect in distal H+ secretion. Urine pH stays >5.5 despite systemic acidosis; plasma K is LOW; FeHCO3 is low (<5%); urine anion gap is POSITIVE; nephrocalcinosis and hypercalciuria are common (alkaline urine promotes calcium phosphate stone formation); treatment is a modest alkali dose (~1-3 mEq/kg/day).","Type 2 (proximal): defect in proximal HCO3 reabsorption. Urine CAN acidify appropriately once serum HCO3 falls low enough (so resting urine pH may be <5.5), but during a bicarbonate-loading test FeHCO3 rises above 15%; plasma K is LOW; nephrocalcinosis is rare; often part of a broader Fanconi syndrome (glucosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia); needs a MUCH larger alkali dose (~5-15 mEq/kg/day) because most of what you give is wasted in the urine, often combined with a thiazide.","Type 4 (hyperkalemic): due to aldosterone deficiency or resistance (or obstructive uropathy/PUV). Plasma K is HIGH (the hallmark that separates it from types 1 and 2); urine anion gap is positive; urine pH is variable, often <5.5; treatment targets the hyperkalemia (dietary K restriction, loop diuretic, fludrocortisone if aldosterone-deficient) — correcting the potassium often corrects the acidosis.","Fanconi syndrome is a generalized proximal tubulopathy: bicarbonaturia (type 2 RTA), glucosuria (with normal blood glucose), aminoaciduria, phosphaturia (with hypophosphatemic rickets), and low-molecular-weight proteinuria — think of it as \"type 2 RTA plus everything else the proximal tubule normally reclaims\".","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus can coexist with or mimic tubular acid-base problems but is a separate water-handling defect (covered in its own Academy topic)."]$kp2$::jsonb,
      presentation = $pres2$Failure to thrive/poor growth is the most common pediatric presentation of any chronic RTA (chronic acidosis impairs growth directly and via bone buffering). Distal RTA classically presents with a kidney stone, nephrocalcinosis on incidental ultrasound, or hypokalemic weakness. Type 4 RTA is often found incidentally on electrolytes in a neonate with obstructive uropathy, or in an older child with an unexplained hyperkalemic, mildly acidotic picture.$pres2$,
      reasoning = $reas2$Step 1: confirm a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Step 2: check plasma potassium — high points to type 4, low points to type 1 or 2. Step 3: separate type 1 from type 2 using urine pH (persistently >5.5 in type 1) and, if needed, a formal FeHCO3 bicarbonate-loading test plus a look for other proximal-tubule losses (glucosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia) that would suggest Fanconi/type 2.$reas2$,
      tests = $tests2$Venous/arterial blood gas, serum electrolytes (anion gap, K), urine pH, urine anion gap, FeHCO3 (formal test requires a sodium-bicarbonate load), urinalysis for glucosuria, and a renal ultrasound to look for nephrocalcinosis (type 1). Genetic testing is available for the inherited forms (e.g. ATP6V0A4, SLC4A1 for distal; SLC4A4 for isolated proximal).$tests2$,
      interpretation = $interp2$Low K + urine pH persistently >5.5 + positive UAG -> distal (type 1). Low K + FeHCO3 >15% during bicarbonate loading +/- other Fanconi features -> proximal (type 2). High K + positive UAG -> type 4 (hypoaldosteronism/obstruction). A negative UAG with a normal-anion-gap acidosis is NOT RTA — look for an extrarenal cause (diarrhea).$interp2$,
      imaging = $img2$Renal ultrasound in suspected distal (type 1) RTA to look for nephrocalcinosis/nephrolithiasis, which is common and can itself cause obstructive complications; not routinely needed for type 2 or type 4 unless an underlying obstructive cause is suspected for type 4.$img2$,
      treatment = $tx2$Type 1: sodium bicarbonate or citrate, ~1-3 mEq/kg/day, titrated to a normal serum HCO3 — this alone typically halts further nephrocalcinosis and improves growth. Type 2: alkali at a much higher dose (~5-15 mEq/kg/day) since urinary losses continue, often plus a thiazide diuretic (mild volume contraction increases proximal HCO3 reabsorption) and potassium supplementation (alkali therapy can worsen hypokalemia). Type 4: treat the hyperkalemia first — dietary potassium restriction, loop diuretic, fludrocortisone if hypoaldosteronism is confirmed, and relieve any underlying obstruction; the acidosis usually improves once potassium normalizes.$tx2$,
      red_flags = $rf2$Don't miss type 4 RTA in a newborn with PUV/obstructive uropathy who looks like they have "poor feeding and vomiting" — the hyperkalemia can be dangerous and is frequently under-recognized as tubular rather than purely due to reduced GFR. In distal RTA, undertreated chronic acidosis silently worsens both linear growth and bone mineralization even when the child looks otherwise well.$rf2$,
      pearls = $pearl2$Board pearl: potassium direction is the fastest type-4-vs-others discriminator (high K = type 4; low K = type 1 or 2). "Nephrocalcinosis + hypokalemia + alkaline urine despite acidosis" = distal (type 1) RTA. "Glucosuria with a normal blood glucose" is a giveaway for Fanconi syndrome/proximal (type 2) RTA.$pearl2$,
      case_stem = $stem2$A 6-month-old boy with posterior urethral valves (status post vesicostomy) has labs: Na 138, K 6.2, Cl 112, HCO3 16 mEq/L, Cr 0.6 mg/dL (above the expected infant baseline). Urine anion gap is positive.$stem2$,
      case_questions = $cq2$["What type of RTA does this picture suggest, and why?","Why is potassium the key discriminator here rather than urine pH?","What is the first step in management?"]$cq2$::jsonb,
      case_discussion = $disc2$This is type 4 (hyperkalemic) RTA secondary to obstructive uropathy (PUV) — the elevated potassium is the hallmark that distinguishes it from type 1 or type 2 RTA, both of which cause hypokalemia. Relieving/confirming adequate drainage of the obstruction and correcting the hyperkalemia (dietary restriction, loop diuretic +/- fludrocortisone if aldosterone response is blunted) typically improves the acidosis without needing aggressive alkali therapy alone.$disc2$
    where id = v_topic_id;
  end if;
end $$;

-- ============================================================
-- 3) Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)
-- ============================================================
do $$
declare
  v_owner_id uuid;
  v_parent_id uuid;
  v_topic_id uuid;
begin
  select id into v_owner_id from auth.users where email = 'alihessamis@gmail.com' limit 1;
  if v_owner_id is null then
    raise exception 'owner not found for email alihessamis@gmail.com';
  end if;

  select id into v_parent_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and category ilike '%AKI%' and parent_topic_id is null
    limit 1;

  select id into v_topic_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%Acute Kidney Injury%'
    limit 1;

  if v_topic_id is null then
    insert into public.academy_topics (
      owner_id, category, parent_topic_id, name, summary, key_points, presentation, reasoning, tests,
      interpretation, imaging, treatment, red_flags, pearls, case_stem, case_questions, case_discussion
    ) values (
      v_owner_id, 'AKI', v_parent_id, 'Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)',
      $sum3$AKI is a sudden decline in GFR causing retention of nitrogenous waste and disrupted fluid/electrolyte/acid-base balance. The classic first split — prerenal vs. intrinsic vs. postrenal — organizes both the differential and the initial workup, and KDIGO staging (already implemented in this app from creatinine, see kdigoStage()) standardizes severity.$sum3$,
      $kp3$["Prerenal AKI (reduced renal perfusion, tubules intact) is the most common cause in children: dehydration/GI losses, hemorrhage, sepsis/capillary leak, heart failure, nephrotic syndrome (intravascular depletion despite edema).","Intrinsic (renal) AKI reflects direct kidney damage: ATN (ischemic or nephrotoxic — aminoglycosides, contrast, NSAIDs, chemotherapy), glomerulonephritis, interstitial nephritis (drug-induced), HUS, and vascular causes (renal vein thrombosis).","Postrenal (obstructive) AKI: PUV, bilateral UPJ/UVJ obstruction, neurogenic bladder, or a single functioning kidney with obstruction — always rule this out first since it's rapidly reversible with drainage.","Table 19.13-style oliguria differentiation: prerenal has FeNa <=1%, BUN/Cr ratio >20:1, urine specific gravity >1.015 (concentrated); intrinsic/ATN has FeNa >2-3%, BUN/Cr ratio <10-15:1, urine SG ~1.010 (isosthenuric, can't concentrate).","BUN/Cr ratio itself is a quick screen: 10-20 is normal, >20 suggests prerenal (disproportionate urea reabsorption with sodium/water), <10 suggests a liver disease process or an inborn error of urea metabolism reducing BUN production independent of GFR.","ATN classically runs three phases: oliguric (days, risk of volume overload/hyperkalemia/acidosis), diuretic (days-weeks, tubules regenerating but not yet concentrating — risk of dehydration/hypokalemia from massive urine losses), and recovery (GFR gradually normalizes, though it can take months and may remain mildly reduced).","Iodinated IV contrast carries a real risk of contrast-induced nephropathy in a patient with reduced GFR; gadolinium-based MRI contrast in advanced CKD/AKI carries a risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis — both should prompt using the lowest effective dose or an alternative study when GFR is significantly reduced."]$kp3$::jsonb,
      $pres3$Oliguria/anuria, edema or volume overload, gross hematuria (if glomerular), vomiting/lethargy from uremia, or an incidentally found elevated creatinine on routine labs. In the hospitalized child, AKI is often first flagged by a rising creatinine trend on daily labs rather than an obvious symptom.$pres3$,
      $reas3$Confirm the baseline creatinine (age/height-expected value if none is on record), stage severity by KDIGO criteria, then work through prerenal -> postrenal -> intrinsic: assess volume status and response to a fluid challenge first, get a bladder/renal ultrasound to exclude obstruction, and if neither explains it, pursue the intrinsic-renal differential (urinalysis for casts/dysmorphic RBCs, complement, ANCA/ANA, medication review for nephrotoxins, hemolysis labs for HUS).$reas3$,
      $tests3$Creatinine trend (vs. baseline or age-expected), BUN, electrolytes (K, HCO3), urinalysis (specific gravity, blood, protein, casts), FeNa/FeUrea, renal ultrasound with Doppler, and — if intrinsic disease is suspected — complement, ANA/ANCA, and hemolysis labs (LDH, haptoglobin, peripheral smear for schistocytes) as indicated by the picture.$tests3$,
      $interp3$KDIGO AKI staging by creatinine: Stage 1 = 1.5-1.9x baseline or a rise >=0.3 mg/dL; Stage 2 = 2-2.9x baseline; Stage 3 = >=3x baseline, Cr >=4.0 mg/dL, or need for renal replacement therapy — this app computes the stage automatically from a recorded baseline Cr. FeNa/FeUrea and BUN/Cr ratio patterns as in the Renal Function Tests topic distinguish prerenal from intrinsic once obstruction is excluded.$interp3$,
      $img3$Renal/bladder ultrasound (with Doppler for renal vein/artery flow when vascular causes are suspected) is the first-line study for every AKI to exclude obstruction and assess kidney size/echogenicity; avoid iodinated contrast and gadolinium unless essential, given the added nephrotoxicity/NSF risk in a kidney already under stress.$img3$,
      $tx3$Treat the cause: fluid resuscitation for prerenal causes (with reassessment, not indefinite fluid pushing), relieve obstruction (catheter/nephrostomy) for postrenal causes, stop nephrotoxins and treat the specific intrinsic disease. Supportive care across all types: strict fluid balance, treat hyperkalemia and acidosis, adjust renally-cleared drug dosing, and initiate dialysis for refractory volume overload, hyperkalemia, severe acidosis, dialyzable toxin ingestion, or uremia (BUN roughly >150 mg/dL or symptomatic).$tx3$,
      $rf3$Don't give a large empiric fluid bolus without first excluding volume overload/heart failure as the actual driver of oliguria — and don't miss postrenal obstruction, since draining it is curative and delay risks permanent damage. The diuretic phase of ATN recovery is a classic point where a child gets re-admitted for dehydration because the massive urine output wasn't anticipated.$rf3$,
      $pearl3$Board pearl: "BUN/Cr ratio >20" with concentrated urine (SG >1.020) and FeNa <1% is prerenal; the same labs with FeNa >2% and isosthenuric urine (SG ~1.010) is ATN. A single kidney (congenital or post-nephrectomy) with a rising creatinine after a febrile illness should raise concern for obstruction of that kidney, not just "dehydration."$pearl3$,
      $stem3$A 4-year-old girl is admitted with 3 days of vomiting and diarrhea. She is tachycardic with dry mucous membranes. Labs: Na 138, K 4.2, Cr 0.9 mg/dL (baseline 0.3), BUN 42 mg/dL. Urinalysis: SG 1.028, no blood/protein. Urine sodium is 8 mEq/L.$stem3$,
      $cq3$["What is this child's KDIGO AKI stage?","Calculate the BUN/Cr ratio and the expected FeNa pattern — what category of AKI does this support?","What is the appropriate first-line treatment, and what would make you reconsider the diagnosis?"]$cq3$::jsonb,
      $disc3$Cr 0.9 vs. baseline 0.3 is a 3x rise -> KDIGO Stage 3. BUN/Cr ratio = 42/0.9 ≈ 47 (>20), concentrated urine (SG 1.028), and a low urine sodium all support a prerenal cause from GI volume losses — an isotonic fluid bolus with reassessment of urine output and creatinine trend is the appropriate first step. Failure of creatinine to improve with adequate volume repletion, or a falling FeNa/BUN-Cr pattern that doesn't correct, should prompt reconsidering an intrinsic process instead.$disc3$
    );
  else
    update public.academy_topics set
      category = 'AKI',
      summary = $sum3$AKI is a sudden decline in GFR causing retention of nitrogenous waste and disrupted fluid/electrolyte/acid-base balance. The classic first split — prerenal vs. intrinsic vs. postrenal — organizes both the differential and the initial workup, and KDIGO staging (already implemented in this app from creatinine, see kdigoStage()) standardizes severity.$sum3$,
      key_points = $kp3$["Prerenal AKI (reduced renal perfusion, tubules intact) is the most common cause in children: dehydration/GI losses, hemorrhage, sepsis/capillary leak, heart failure, nephrotic syndrome (intravascular depletion despite edema).","Intrinsic (renal) AKI reflects direct kidney damage: ATN (ischemic or nephrotoxic — aminoglycosides, contrast, NSAIDs, chemotherapy), glomerulonephritis, interstitial nephritis (drug-induced), HUS, and vascular causes (renal vein thrombosis).","Postrenal (obstructive) AKI: PUV, bilateral UPJ/UVJ obstruction, neurogenic bladder, or a single functioning kidney with obstruction — always rule this out first since it's rapidly reversible with drainage.","Table 19.13-style oliguria differentiation: prerenal has FeNa <=1%, BUN/Cr ratio >20:1, urine specific gravity >1.015 (concentrated); intrinsic/ATN has FeNa >2-3%, BUN/Cr ratio <10-15:1, urine SG ~1.010 (isosthenuric, can't concentrate).","BUN/Cr ratio itself is a quick screen: 10-20 is normal, >20 suggests prerenal (disproportionate urea reabsorption with sodium/water), <10 suggests a liver disease process or an inborn error of urea metabolism reducing BUN production independent of GFR.","ATN classically runs three phases: oliguric (days, risk of volume overload/hyperkalemia/acidosis), diuretic (days-weeks, tubules regenerating but not yet concentrating — risk of dehydration/hypokalemia from massive urine losses), and recovery (GFR gradually normalizes, though it can take months and may remain mildly reduced).","Iodinated IV contrast carries a real risk of contrast-induced nephropathy in a patient with reduced GFR; gadolinium-based MRI contrast in advanced CKD/AKI carries a risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis — both should prompt using the lowest effective dose or an alternative study when GFR is significantly reduced."]$kp3$::jsonb,
      presentation = $pres3$Oliguria/anuria, edema or volume overload, gross hematuria (if glomerular), vomiting/lethargy from uremia, or an incidentally found elevated creatinine on routine labs. In the hospitalized child, AKI is often first flagged by a rising creatinine trend on daily labs rather than an obvious symptom.$pres3$,
      reasoning = $reas3$Confirm the baseline creatinine (age/height-expected value if none is on record), stage severity by KDIGO criteria, then work through prerenal -> postrenal -> intrinsic: assess volume status and response to a fluid challenge first, get a bladder/renal ultrasound to exclude obstruction, and if neither explains it, pursue the intrinsic-renal differential (urinalysis for casts/dysmorphic RBCs, complement, ANCA/ANA, medication review for nephrotoxins, hemolysis labs for HUS).$reas3$,
      tests = $tests3$Creatinine trend (vs. baseline or age-expected), BUN, electrolytes (K, HCO3), urinalysis (specific gravity, blood, protein, casts), FeNa/FeUrea, renal ultrasound with Doppler, and — if intrinsic disease is suspected — complement, ANA/ANCA, and hemolysis labs (LDH, haptoglobin, peripheral smear for schistocytes) as indicated by the picture.$tests3$,
      interpretation = $interp3$KDIGO AKI staging by creatinine: Stage 1 = 1.5-1.9x baseline or a rise >=0.3 mg/dL; Stage 2 = 2-2.9x baseline; Stage 3 = >=3x baseline, Cr >=4.0 mg/dL, or need for renal replacement therapy — this app computes the stage automatically from a recorded baseline Cr. FeNa/FeUrea and BUN/Cr ratio patterns as in the Renal Function Tests topic distinguish prerenal from intrinsic once obstruction is excluded.$interp3$,
      imaging = $img3$Renal/bladder ultrasound (with Doppler for renal vein/artery flow when vascular causes are suspected) is the first-line study for every AKI to exclude obstruction and assess kidney size/echogenicity; avoid iodinated contrast and gadolinium unless essential, given the added nephrotoxicity/NSF risk in a kidney already under stress.$img3$,
      treatment = $tx3$Treat the cause: fluid resuscitation for prerenal causes (with reassessment, not indefinite fluid pushing), relieve obstruction (catheter/nephrostomy) for postrenal causes, stop nephrotoxins and treat the specific intrinsic disease. Supportive care across all types: strict fluid balance, treat hyperkalemia and acidosis, adjust renally-cleared drug dosing, and initiate dialysis for refractory volume overload, hyperkalemia, severe acidosis, dialyzable toxin ingestion, or uremia (BUN roughly >150 mg/dL or symptomatic).$tx3$,
      red_flags = $rf3$Don't give a large empiric fluid bolus without first excluding volume overload/heart failure as the actual driver of oliguria — and don't miss postrenal obstruction, since draining it is curative and delay risks permanent damage. The diuretic phase of ATN recovery is a classic point where a child gets re-admitted for dehydration because the massive urine output wasn't anticipated.$rf3$,
      pearls = $pearl3$Board pearl: "BUN/Cr ratio >20" with concentrated urine (SG >1.020) and FeNa <1% is prerenal; the same labs with FeNa >2% and isosthenuric urine (SG ~1.010) is ATN. A single kidney (congenital or post-nephrectomy) with a rising creatinine after a febrile illness should raise concern for obstruction of that kidney, not just "dehydration."$pearl3$,
      case_stem = $stem3$A 4-year-old girl is admitted with 3 days of vomiting and diarrhea. She is tachycardic with dry mucous membranes. Labs: Na 138, K 4.2, Cr 0.9 mg/dL (baseline 0.3), BUN 42 mg/dL. Urinalysis: SG 1.028, no blood/protein. Urine sodium is 8 mEq/L.$stem3$,
