-- ============================================================
-- 4) CKD: Staging & Systemic Manifestations
-- ============================================================
do $$
declare
  v_owner_id uuid;
  v_parent_id uuid;
  v_topic_id uuid;
begin
  select id into v_owner_id from auth.users where email = 'alihessamis@gmail.com' limit 1;
  if v_owner_id is null then
    raise exception 'owner not found for email alihessamis@gmail.com';
  end if;

  select id into v_parent_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and category ilike '%CKD%' and parent_topic_id is null
    limit 1;

  select id into v_topic_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%CKD%Staging%'
    limit 1;

  if v_topic_id is null then
    insert into public.academy_topics (
      owner_id, category, parent_topic_id, name, summary, key_points, presentation, reasoning, tests,
      interpretation, imaging, treatment, red_flags, pearls, case_stem, case_questions, case_discussion
    ) values (
      v_owner_id, 'CKD', v_parent_id, 'CKD: Staging & Systemic Manifestations',
      $sum4$CKD is kidney damage or reduced GFR persisting >3 months. Its five-stage classification (this app computes it automatically via ckdStage()) predicts which systemic complications to screen for, and the etiology mix is different from adults: congenital anomalies (CAKUT) dominate in young children, while acquired glomerular disease becomes more common in older children and adolescents. (CKD-MBD, the bone/mineral piece of this, has its own dedicated Academy topic — this one covers the rest of the systemic picture.)$sum4$,
      $kp4$["Definition: kidney damage (structural or functional abnormality, e.g. persistent proteinuria/hematuria/imaging abnormality) OR GFR <60 mL/min/1.73m^2, present for >=3 months.","KDIGO staging by GFR (mL/min/1.73m^2): Stage 1 >=90 (damage present, normal/high GFR), Stage 2 60-89 (mild decrease), Stage 3a 45-59, Stage 3b 30-44, Stage 4 15-29 (severe), Stage 5 <15 (kidney failure/needs renal replacement therapy).","Etiology skews by age: infants and young children — CAKUT (renal hypoplasia/dysplasia, obstructive uropathy/PUV, reflux nephropathy) account for roughly half of pediatric CKD; older children/adolescents — acquired glomerular disease (FSGS, chronic GN, lupus nephritis) and hereditary conditions (Alport syndrome, cystic kidney disease) become proportionally more common.","Growth failure is one of the most characteristic pediatric CKD manifestations — driven by chronic acidosis, anorexia/caloric insufficiency, renal osteodystrophy, and resistance to growth hormone; recombinant GH therapy is used once nutrition and acidosis are optimized.","Anemia of CKD develops from reduced erythropoietin production plus a degree of iron-deficiency/inflammation — screened with ferritin/iron/TIBC and treated with iron repletion +/- erythropoiesis-stimulating agents once other causes are excluded.","Hypertension and cardiovascular disease risk rise as GFR falls — children with CKD have markedly increased long-term cardiovascular mortality risk compared to age-matched peers, driven by chronic volume overload, secondary hyperparathyroidism/vascular calcification, and dyslipidemia; BP control is a core CKD-progression-slowing intervention.","Metabolic acidosis and electrolyte disturbances (hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia) become more prominent as GFR falls below ~30 (Stage 4-5) and need to be watched at every visit even when the child feels well."]$kp4$::jsonb,
      $pres4$Often asymptomatic and found on screening labs/prenatal ultrasound findings (e.g. antenatal hydronephrosis leading to a CAKUT diagnosis) rather than presenting acutely. When symptomatic: growth failure/poor weight gain, fatigue (from anemia), polyuria/polydipsia (impaired concentrating ability), bone pain/deformity (renal osteodystrophy), or incidental hypertension.$pres4$,
      $reas4$Once CKD is identified, stage it by GFR, determine the likely cause (ultrasound + urinalysis + family history first, since CAKUT and hereditary disease are so common in children), and start a structured surveillance plan: growth parameters at every visit, periodic anemia and CKD-MBD labs (see the CKD periodic screening reminder already built into this app's Labs tab), blood pressure at every visit, and referral for transplant evaluation planning well before Stage 5 is reached.$reas4$,
      $tests4$Serum creatinine/eGFR trend, urinalysis and urine protein/creatinine ratio, renal ultrasound, blood pressure, CBC + iron studies (anemia), and the CKD-MBD panel (calcium, phosphorus, PTH, 25-OH vitamin D, alkaline phosphatase, bicarbonate) — all already tracked elsewhere in this app.$tests4$,
      $interp4$Stage by GFR as above; a falling GFR trajectory (not just the current stage) is what actually predicts time to kidney failure, so trend matters more than any single value. New or worsening proteinuria, hypertension, or anemia at a given stage suggests either disease progression or a superimposed process (e.g. an AKI-on-CKD event) rather than "expected" CKD.$interp4$,
      $img4$Renal ultrasound at diagnosis and periodically to track kidney size/echogenicity/scarring, and a VCUG if reflux nephropathy is on the differential; echocardiography is often added once significant hypertension or advanced CKD raises concern for left ventricular hypertrophy.$img4$,
      $tx4$Multidisciplinary, stage-based management: renin-angiotensin system blockade (ACEi/ARB) for proteinuric CKD to slow progression (with careful potassium/creatinine monitoring), blood pressure control to an appropriate pediatric target, nutrition optimization (caloric intake, sometimes a controlled-protein approach), alkali therapy for acidosis, anemia management, CKD-MBD management (see that topic), growth hormone if growth failure persists despite the above, and timely transplant evaluation as Stage 4-5 approaches (living-donor pre-emptive transplant avoids dialysis altogether when feasible).$tx4$,
      $rf4$Don't attribute a child's poor growth or fatigue to "just being a kid with CKD" without actively screening for the treatable contributors (acidosis, anemia, inadequate calories, uncontrolled secondary hyperparathyroidism) — each has a specific, correctable therapy. A rapid, unexplained drop in GFR should prompt looking for a reversible superimposed insult (dehydration, nephrotoxin, obstruction) rather than assuming pure disease progression.$rf4$,
      $pearl4$Board pearl: in a young child, CAKUT (renal hypoplasia/dysplasia, obstructive uropathy) is the single most likely cause of CKD on an exam question; in an adolescent, think acquired glomerular disease or a hereditary nephropathy (Alport, ADPKD) instead. "Antenatal hydronephrosis" + "poor growth" + "polyuria" in an infant is a classic CAKUT/CKD vignette.$pearl4$,
      $stem4$A 2-year-old boy, born with a history of antenatal bilateral hydronephrosis, is noted to have poor weight gain and is drinking unusually large volumes of water. Labs: Cr 0.7 mg/dL (elevated for age), HCO3 18 mEq/L, Hgb 9.8 g/dL. Renal ultrasound shows small, echogenic kidneys with cortical thinning.$stem4$,
      $cq4$["What is the most likely underlying cause of this child's CKD given the antenatal history?","Using the eGFR/CKD staging framework, what work-up would you prioritize at this visit?","Name three treatable contributors to his poor growth that should be addressed before considering growth hormone."]$cq4$::jsonb,
      $disc4$The antenatal bilateral hydronephrosis, small echogenic kidneys, and polyuria (impaired concentrating ability) point to a CAKUT etiology (renal hypoplasia/dysplasia +/- obstructive uropathy) — the leading cause of pediatric CKD in this age group. At this visit: calculate/stage eGFR, send a CKD-MBD panel and iron studies, and check blood pressure. Before considering growth hormone, correct the metabolic acidosis (HCO3 18 is below target), treat the anemia (Hgb 9.8), and ensure caloric intake is adequate — all three directly impair growth and are treated first.$disc4$
    );
  else
    update public.academy_topics set
      category = 'CKD',
      summary = $sum4$CKD is kidney damage or reduced GFR persisting >3 months. Its five-stage classification (this app computes it automatically via ckdStage()) predicts which systemic complications to screen for, and the etiology mix is different from adults: congenital anomalies (CAKUT) dominate in young children, while acquired glomerular disease becomes more common in older children and adolescents. (CKD-MBD, the bone/mineral piece of this, has its own dedicated Academy topic — this one covers the rest of the systemic picture.)$sum4$,
      key_points = $kp4$["Definition: kidney damage (structural or functional abnormality, e.g. persistent proteinuria/hematuria/imaging abnormality) OR GFR <60 mL/min/1.73m^2, present for >=3 months.","KDIGO staging by GFR (mL/min/1.73m^2): Stage 1 >=90 (damage present, normal/high GFR), Stage 2 60-89 (mild decrease), Stage 3a 45-59, Stage 3b 30-44, Stage 4 15-29 (severe), Stage 5 <15 (kidney failure/needs renal replacement therapy).","Etiology skews by age: infants and young children — CAKUT (renal hypoplasia/dysplasia, obstructive uropathy/PUV, reflux nephropathy) account for roughly half of pediatric CKD; older children/adolescents — acquired glomerular disease (FSGS, chronic GN, lupus nephritis) and hereditary conditions (Alport syndrome, cystic kidney disease) become proportionally more common.","Growth failure is one of the most characteristic pediatric CKD manifestations — driven by chronic acidosis, anorexia/caloric insufficiency, renal osteodystrophy, and resistance to growth hormone; recombinant GH therapy is used once nutrition and acidosis are optimized.","Anemia of CKD develops from reduced erythropoietin production plus a degree of iron-deficiency/inflammation — screened with ferritin/iron/TIBC and treated with iron repletion +/- erythropoiesis-stimulating agents once other causes are excluded.","Hypertension and cardiovascular disease risk rise as GFR falls — children with CKD have markedly increased long-term cardiovascular mortality risk compared to age-matched peers, driven by chronic volume overload, secondary hyperparathyroidism/vascular calcification, and dyslipidemia; BP control is a core CKD-progression-slowing intervention.","Metabolic acidosis and electrolyte disturbances (hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia) become more prominent as GFR falls below ~30 (Stage 4-5) and need to be watched at every visit even when the child feels well."]$kp4$::jsonb,
      presentation = $pres4$Often asymptomatic and found on screening labs/prenatal ultrasound findings (e.g. antenatal hydronephrosis leading to a CAKUT diagnosis) rather than presenting acutely. When symptomatic: growth failure/poor weight gain, fatigue (from anemia), polyuria/polydipsia (impaired concentrating ability), bone pain/deformity (renal osteodystrophy), or incidental hypertension.$pres4$,
      reasoning = $reas4$Once CKD is identified, stage it by GFR, determine the likely cause (ultrasound + urinalysis + family history first, since CAKUT and hereditary disease are so common in children), and start a structured surveillance plan: growth parameters at every visit, periodic anemia and CKD-MBD labs (see the CKD periodic screening reminder already built into this app's Labs tab), blood pressure at every visit, and referral for transplant evaluation planning well before Stage 5 is reached.$reas4$,
      tests = $tests4$Serum creatinine/eGFR trend, urinalysis and urine protein/creatinine ratio, renal ultrasound, blood pressure, CBC + iron studies (anemia), and the CKD-MBD panel (calcium, phosphorus, PTH, 25-OH vitamin D, alkaline phosphatase, bicarbonate) — all already tracked elsewhere in this app.$tests4$,
      interpretation = $interp4$Stage by GFR as above; a falling GFR trajectory (not just the current stage) is what actually predicts time to kidney failure, so trend matters more than any single value. New or worsening proteinuria, hypertension, or anemia at a given stage suggests either disease progression or a superimposed process (e.g. an AKI-on-CKD event) rather than "expected" CKD.$interp4$,
      imaging = $img4$Renal ultrasound at diagnosis and periodically to track kidney size/echogenicity/scarring, and a VCUG if reflux nephropathy is on the differential; echocardiography is often added once significant hypertension or advanced CKD raises concern for left ventricular hypertrophy.$img4$,
      treatment = $tx4$Multidisciplinary, stage-based management: renin-angiotensin system blockade (ACEi/ARB) for proteinuric CKD to slow progression (with careful potassium/creatinine monitoring), blood pressure control to an appropriate pediatric target, nutrition optimization (caloric intake, sometimes a controlled-protein approach), alkali therapy for acidosis, anemia management, CKD-MBD management (see that topic), growth hormone if growth failure persists despite the above, and timely transplant evaluation as Stage 4-5 approaches (living-donor pre-emptive transplant avoids dialysis altogether when feasible).$tx4$,
      red_flags = $rf4$Don't attribute a child's poor growth or fatigue to "just being a kid with CKD" without actively screening for the treatable contributors (acidosis, anemia, inadequate calories, uncontrolled secondary hyperparathyroidism) — each has a specific, correctable therapy. A rapid, unexplained drop in GFR should prompt looking for a reversible superimposed insult (dehydration, nephrotoxin, obstruction) rather than assuming pure disease progression.$rf4$,
      pearls = $pearl4$Board pearl: in a young child, CAKUT (renal hypoplasia/dysplasia, obstructive uropathy) is the single most likely cause of CKD on an exam question; in an adolescent, think acquired glomerular disease or a hereditary nephropathy (Alport, ADPKD) instead. "Antenatal hydronephrosis" + "poor growth" + "polyuria" in an infant is a classic CAKUT/CKD vignette.$pearl4$,
      case_stem = $stem4$A 2-year-old boy, born with a history of antenatal bilateral hydronephrosis, is noted to have poor weight gain and is drinking unusually large volumes of water. Labs: Cr 0.7 mg/dL (elevated for age), HCO3 18 mEq/L, Hgb 9.8 g/dL. Renal ultrasound shows small, echogenic kidneys with cortical thinning.$stem4$,
      case_questions = $cq4$["What is the most likely underlying cause of this child's CKD given the antenatal history?","Using the eGFR/CKD staging framework, what work-up would you prioritize at this visit?","Name three treatable contributors to his poor growth that should be addressed before considering growth hormone."]$cq4$::jsonb,
      case_discussion = $disc4$The antenatal bilateral hydronephrosis, small echogenic kidneys, and polyuria (impaired concentrating ability) point to a CAKUT etiology (renal hypoplasia/dysplasia +/- obstructive uropathy) — the leading cause of pediatric CKD in this age group. At this visit: calculate/stage eGFR, send a CKD-MBD panel and iron studies, and check blood pressure. Before considering growth hormone, correct the metabolic acidosis (HCO3 18 is below target), treat the anemia (Hgb 9.8), and ensure caloric intake is adequate — all three directly impair growth and are treated first.$disc4$
    where id = v_topic_id;
  end if;
end $$;

-- ============================================================
-- 5) UTI & Vesicoureteral Reflux (VUR)
-- ============================================================
do $$
declare
  v_owner_id uuid;
  v_parent_id uuid;
  v_topic_id uuid;
begin
  select id into v_owner_id from auth.users where email = 'alihessamis@gmail.com' limit 1;
  if v_owner_id is null then
    raise exception 'owner not found for email alihessamis@gmail.com';
  end if;

  select id into v_parent_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and category ilike '%UTI%' and parent_topic_id is null
    limit 1;

  select id into v_topic_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%Vesicoureteral Reflux%' or (owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%UTI%VUR%')
    limit 1;

  if v_topic_id is null then
    insert into public.academy_topics (
      owner_id, category, parent_topic_id, name, summary, key_points, presentation, reasoning, tests,
      interpretation, imaging, treatment, red_flags, pearls, case_stem, case_questions, case_discussion
    ) values (
      v_owner_id, 'UTI & VUR', v_parent_id, 'UTI & Vesicoureteral Reflux (VUR)',
      $sum5$Pediatric UTI diagnosis hinges on a properly collected, culture-confirmed specimen rather than dipstick alone, and the post-UTI imaging workup exists to find the children who actually need it — those at risk for VUR and renal scarring — without over-imaging everyone else.$sum5$,
      $kp5$["Dipstick screening: leukocyte esterase is sensitive but not specific (can be positive with any inflammation); nitrite is specific but insensitive (requires several hours of bladder dwell time and only some organisms convert nitrate to nitrite) — a negative nitrite does NOT rule out UTI, especially in a frequently-voiding infant.","Pyuria is defined as >=5 WBC/hpf on microscopy; a urine culture is required to confirm infection, and the colony-count threshold for a true positive depends on collection method: clean catch generally >=100,000 CFU/mL, catheterized specimen >=50,000 CFU/mL (some use >=10,000), suprapubic aspirate ANY growth of a single organism is significant.","Pyelonephritis (upper tract) vs. cystitis (lower tract, bladder only) is distinguished clinically: fever (especially >38.5C), flank pain/CVA tenderness, and systemic symptoms suggest pyelonephritis; dysuria/frequency/urgency without fever suggests cystitis — this distinction drives both imaging decisions and treatment duration.","Asymptomatic bacteriuria (positive culture without pyuria/symptoms) should NOT be treated — an important board distractor.","VUR grading (International Reflux Study Grades I-V) is assessed by VCUG: I = reflux into a non-dilated ureter only; II = reflux into the pelvis/calyces without dilation; III = mild ureteral/pelvicalyceal dilation; IV = moderate ureteral tortuosity/dilation with blunted calyces; V = gross dilation and tortuosity with loss of papillary impressions — higher grades carry higher risk of renal scarring and lower rates of spontaneous resolution.","Imaging after a first febrile UTI: renal/bladder ultrasound is reasonable after most first febrile UTIs to look for anatomic abnormalities/hydronephrosis; a VCUG is reserved for atypical/recurrent UTI, an abnormal ultrasound, or clinical concern for high-grade reflux — not obtained reflexively after every first febrile UTI in an otherwise well-appearing infant with a normal ultrasound (current AAP-aligned practice, a shift from older reflexive-VCUG teaching).","Treatment duration: uncomplicated cystitis 3-5 days; febrile UTI/pyelonephritis 7-14 days (oral therapy is appropriate for most non-toxic-appearing children who can tolerate PO); antibiotic choice should be narrowed once culture and sensitivities return."]$kp5$::jsonb,
      $pres5$Infants: fever without an obvious source is the most common presentation — UTI should be on the differential for any febrile infant, especially uncircumcised males and females under 2 years. Older children: dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain (cystitis) or fever, flank pain, vomiting (pyelonephritis). VUR itself is usually silent and found only through the post-UTI workup or antenatal hydronephrosis screening, not through its own symptoms.$pres5$,
      $reas5$Obtain a properly collected specimen (catheterized or suprapubic in a non-toilet-trained child; clean catch in an older child) before starting antibiotics whenever feasible — a bagged specimen is only useful to exclude UTI (high false-positive rate) not to confirm it. Distinguish cystitis from pyelonephritis clinically to guide imaging and duration. After a febrile UTI, decide on ultrasound (most) vs. VCUG (selectively, per the criteria above) based on response to treatment, recurrence, and ultrasound findings.$reas5$,
      $tests5$Urinalysis (pyuria, nitrite, leukocyte esterase) plus a culture from an appropriately collected specimen; renal/bladder ultrasound; VCUG when indicated; DMSA renal scan is used in some centers to detect acute pyelonephritis or chronic scarring when the diagnosis or its renal impact is unclear.$tests5$,
      $interp5$Positive nitrite is highly specific for UTI; a negative nitrite with positive leukocyte esterase and pyuria is still consistent with UTI. Colony counts are interpreted against the collection-method-specific thresholds above — a low count on a bagged specimen is not diagnostic. VUR grade on VCUG predicts scarring risk and spontaneous resolution likelihood (lower grades resolve spontaneously far more often than grade IV-V).$interp5$,
      $img5$Renal/bladder ultrasound after most first febrile UTIs (looks for hydronephrosis, duplication, scarring, bladder abnormalities); VCUG reserved for atypical/recurrent UTI, an abnormal ultrasound, or a strong clinical suspicion of high-grade VUR; DMSA scan selectively for acute pyelonephritis confirmation or scar detection.$img5$,
      $tx5$Empiric antibiotics pending culture, narrowed once sensitivities return; oral therapy for most non-toxic children able to tolerate it, IV therapy for infants <2-3 months, toxic-appearing children, or those unable to tolerate PO. Duration: 3-5 days cystitis, 7-14 days febrile UTI/pyelonephritis. VUR management is graded: low-grade VUR is often managed with observation +/- continuous antibiotic prophylaxis and expectant monitoring for spontaneous resolution; higher-grade VUR with breakthrough UTIs or scarring may warrant surgical correction (ureteral reimplantation or endoscopic injection).$tx5$,
      $rf5$Don't treat asymptomatic bacteriuria. Don't rely on a bagged urine specimen to diagnose (only to screen out) UTI. In a young febrile infant, a negative nitrite does not exclude UTI — send a culture from a proper specimen regardless. Recurrent febrile UTIs despite a normal initial ultrasound still warrant reconsidering VCUG.$rf5$,
      $pearl5$Board pearl: "fever without a source in an infant" = check a catheterized urine culture, don't just dipstick a bag specimen. "Positive culture, no pyuria, no symptoms" = asymptomatic bacteriuria, do not treat. VUR grade correlates with scarring risk — grade V is the one you worry most about for renal damage and hypertension/CKD long-term.$pearl5$,
      $stem5$A 4-month-old uncircumcised male presents with fever of 39.2C and no other symptoms. A catheterized urine sample shows pyuria and is sent for culture, which grows >100,000 CFU/mL of E. coli.$stem5$,
      $cq5$["What is the appropriate initial antibiotic approach and duration for this presentation?","What imaging, if any, should be obtained after this first febrile UTI?","If a VCUG is eventually performed and shows reflux into a mildly dilated ureter and pelvis without calyceal blunting, what VUR grade is this and how would it typically be managed?"]$cq5$::jsonb,
      $disc5$This is a febrile UTI (pyelonephritis given the fever) — treat for 7-14 days, oral antibiotics are appropriate if he is not toxic-appearing and tolerating PO, narrowed per culture sensitivities. A renal/bladder ultrasound is reasonable after this first febrile UTI; VCUG would only be pursued if the ultrasound is abnormal, the UTI recurs, or the clinical course is atypical. The described VCUG finding (dilated ureter and pelvis without calyceal blunting) is Grade III VUR — typically managed with observation or continuous antibiotic prophylaxis and monitoring, since many grade III cases resolve spontaneously, escalating to surgical management only with breakthrough UTIs or evidence of new scarring.$disc5$
    );
  else
    update public.academy_topics set
      category = 'UTI & VUR',
      summary = $sum5$Pediatric UTI diagnosis hinges on a properly collected, culture-confirmed specimen rather than dipstick alone, and the post-UTI imaging workup exists to find the children who actually need it — those at risk for VUR and renal scarring — without over-imaging everyone else.$sum5$,
      key_points = $kp5$["Dipstick screening: leukocyte esterase is sensitive but not specific (can be positive with any inflammation); nitrite is specific but insensitive (requires several hours of bladder dwell time and only some organisms convert nitrate to nitrite) — a negative nitrite does NOT rule out UTI, especially in a frequently-voiding infant.","Pyuria is defined as >=5 WBC/hpf on microscopy; a urine culture is required to confirm infection, and the colony-count threshold for a true positive depends on collection method: clean catch generally >=100,000 CFU/mL, catheterized specimen >=50,000 CFU/mL (some use >=10,000), suprapubic aspirate ANY growth of a single organism is significant.","Pyelonephritis (upper tract) vs. cystitis (lower tract, bladder only) is distinguished clinically: fever (especially >38.5C), flank pain/CVA tenderness, and systemic symptoms suggest pyelonephritis; dysuria/frequency/urgency without fever suggests cystitis — this distinction drives both imaging decisions and treatment duration.","Asymptomatic bacteriuria (positive culture without pyuria/symptoms) should NOT be treated — an important board distractor.","VUR grading (International Reflux Study Grades I-V) is assessed by VCUG: I = reflux into a non-dilated ureter only; II = reflux into the pelvis/calyces without dilation; III = mild ureteral/pelvicalyceal dilation; IV = moderate ureteral tortuosity/dilation with blunted calyces; V = gross dilation and tortuosity with loss of papillary impressions — higher grades carry higher risk of renal scarring and lower rates of spontaneous resolution.","Imaging after a first febrile UTI: renal/bladder ultrasound is reasonable after most first febrile UTIs to look for anatomic abnormalities/hydronephrosis; a VCUG is reserved for atypical/recurrent UTI, an abnormal ultrasound, or clinical concern for high-grade reflux — not obtained reflexively after every first febrile UTI in an otherwise well-appearing infant with a normal ultrasound (current AAP-aligned practice, a shift from older reflexive-VCUG teaching).","Treatment duration: uncomplicated cystitis 3-5 days; febrile UTI/pyelonephritis 7-14 days (oral therapy is appropriate for most non-toxic-appearing children who can tolerate PO); antibiotic choice should be narrowed once culture and sensitivities return."]$kp5$::jsonb,
      presentation = $pres5$Infants: fever without an obvious source is the most common presentation — UTI should be on the differential for any febrile infant, especially uncircumcised males and females under 2 years. Older children: dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain (cystitis) or fever, flank pain, vomiting (pyelonephritis). VUR itself is usually silent and found only through the post-UTI workup or antenatal hydronephrosis screening, not through its own symptoms.$pres5$,
      reasoning = $reas5$Obtain a properly collected specimen (catheterized or suprapubic in a non-toilet-trained child; clean catch in an older child) before starting antibiotics whenever feasible — a bagged specimen is only useful to exclude UTI (high false-positive rate) not to confirm it. Distinguish cystitis from pyelonephritis clinically to guide imaging and duration. After a febrile UTI, decide on ultrasound (most) vs. VCUG (selectively, per the criteria above) based on response to treatment, recurrence, and ultrasound findings.$reas5$,
      tests = $tests5$Urinalysis (pyuria, nitrite, leukocyte esterase) plus a culture from an appropriately collected specimen; renal/bladder ultrasound; VCUG when indicated; DMSA renal scan is used in some centers to detect acute pyelonephritis or chronic scarring when the diagnosis or its renal impact is unclear.$tests5$,
      interpretation = $interp5$Positive nitrite is highly specific for UTI; a negative nitrite with positive leukocyte esterase and pyuria is still consistent with UTI. Colony counts are interpreted against the collection-method-specific thresholds above — a low count on a bagged specimen is not diagnostic. VUR grade on VCUG predicts scarring risk and spontaneous resolution likelihood (lower grades resolve spontaneously far more often than grade IV-V).$interp5$,
      imaging = $img5$Renal/bladder ultrasound after most first febrile UTIs (looks for hydronephrosis, duplication, scarring, bladder abnormalities); VCUG reserved for atypical/recurrent UTI, an abnormal ultrasound, or a strong clinical suspicion of high-grade VUR; DMSA scan selectively for acute pyelonephritis confirmation or scar detection.$img5$,
      treatment = $tx5$Empiric antibiotics pending culture, narrowed once sensitivities return; oral therapy for most non-toxic children able to tolerate it, IV therapy for infants <2-3 months, toxic-appearing children, or those unable to tolerate PO. Duration: 3-5 days cystitis, 7-14 days febrile UTI/pyelonephritis. VUR management is graded: low-grade VUR is often managed with observation +/- continuous antibiotic prophylaxis and expectant monitoring for spontaneous resolution; higher-grade VUR with breakthrough UTIs or scarring may warrant surgical correction (ureteral reimplantation or endoscopic injection).$tx5$,
      red_flags = $rf5$Don't treat asymptomatic bacteriuria. Don't rely on a bagged urine specimen to diagnose (only to screen out) UTI. In a young febrile infant, a negative nitrite does not exclude UTI — send a culture from a proper specimen regardless. Recurrent febrile UTIs despite a normal initial ultrasound still warrant reconsidering VCUG.$rf5$,
      pearls = $pearl5$Board pearl: "fever without a source in an infant" = check a catheterized urine culture, don't just dipstick a bag specimen. "Positive culture, no pyuria, no symptoms" = asymptomatic bacteriuria, do not treat. VUR grade correlates with scarring risk — grade V is the one you worry most about for renal damage and hypertension/CKD long-term.$pearl5$,
      case_stem = $stem5$A 4-month-old uncircumcised male presents with fever of 39.2C and no other symptoms. A catheterized urine sample shows pyuria and is sent for culture, which grows >100,000 CFU/mL of E. coli.$stem5$,
      case_questions = $cq5$["What is the appropriate initial antibiotic approach and duration for this presentation?","What imaging, if any, should be obtained after this first febrile UTI?","If a VCUG is eventually performed and shows reflux into a mildly dilated ureter and pelvis without calyceal blunting, what VUR grade is this and how would it typically be managed?"]$cq5$::jsonb,
      case_discussion = $disc5$This is a febrile UTI (pyelonephritis given the fever) — treat for 7-14 days, oral antibiotics are appropriate if he is not toxic-appearing and tolerating PO, narrowed per culture sensitivities. A renal/bladder ultrasound is reasonable after this first febrile UTI; VCUG would only be pursued if the ultrasound is abnormal, the UTI recurs, or the clinical course is atypical. The described VCUG finding (dilated ureter and pelvis without calyceal blunting) is Grade III VUR — typically managed with observation or continuous antibiotic prophylaxis and monitoring, since many grade III cases resolve spontaneously, escalating to surgical management only with breakthrough UTIs or evidence of new scarring.$disc5$
