-- ============================================================
-- 6) Nephrolithiasis
-- ============================================================
do $$
declare
  v_owner_id uuid;
  v_parent_id uuid;
  v_topic_id uuid;
begin
  select id into v_owner_id from auth.users where email = 'alihessamis@gmail.com' limit 1;
  if v_owner_id is null then
    raise exception 'owner not found for email alihessamis@gmail.com';
  end if;

  select id into v_parent_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and category ilike '%Nephrolithiasis%' and parent_topic_id is null
    limit 1;

  select id into v_topic_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%Nephrolithiasis%'
    limit 1;

  if v_topic_id is null then
    insert into public.academy_topics (
      owner_id, category, parent_topic_id, name, summary, key_points, presentation, reasoning, tests,
      interpretation, imaging, treatment, red_flags, pearls, case_stem, case_questions, case_discussion
    ) values (
      v_owner_id, 'Nephrolithiasis', v_parent_id, 'Nephrolithiasis',
      $sum6$Pediatric kidney stones are rising in incidence and, unlike in adults, up to 75% of children have an identifiable underlying metabolic abnormality — so every pediatric stone deserves a real metabolic workup, not just symptomatic management and reassurance.$sum6$,
      $kp6$["Risk factors: hypercalciuria (the most common metabolic abnormality found), hypocitraturia, hyperoxaluria, cystinuria, uric acid stones, urinary tract abnormalities/stasis (UPJ obstruction, VUR, neurogenic bladder), recurrent UTIs with urease-producing organisms (struvite stones), low fluid intake, and a family history of stones.","Presentation: classic renal colic (severe flank pain radiating to the groin) is less reliable in young children, who more often present with vague abdominal pain, gross or microscopic hematuria, or an incidental finding on imaging obtained for another reason.","First-line imaging is renal/bladder ultrasound (no radiation, good sensitivity for larger stones and hydronephrosis); non-contrast CT is more sensitive for small/ureteral stones but is reserved for cases where ultrasound is non-diagnostic and the diagnosis will change management, given radiation exposure in children.","Metabolic workup after a first stone: spot and/or 24-hour urine \"stone risk analysis\" — calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, cystine, sodium, creatinine — plus serum electrolytes, calcium, phosphorus, PTH, and uric acid; stone composition analysis if a stone is recovered.","Hypercalciuria workup uses the age-adjusted spot urine calcium/creatinine ratio (same cutoffs as in the Renal Function Tests topic: <7mo 0.86, 7-18mo 0.60, 19mo-6y 0.42, >6y 0.22 mg/mg) or a 24-hour urine calcium >4 mg/kg/day.","General prevention across almost all stone types: increased fluid intake (the single most broadly effective intervention), dietary sodium moderation (high sodium drives urinary calcium excretion), and targeted therapy for the specific abnormality found (thiazide for hypercalciuria, potassium citrate for hypocitraturia/to alkalinize urine, allopurinol for hyperuricosuria, cystine-binding agents for cystinuria).","Urologic intervention (lithotripsy, ureteroscopy, percutaneous nephrolithotomy) is reserved for stones causing obstruction, intractable pain, infection, or that fail to pass — most small distal stones are managed expectantly with hydration and pain control while awaiting spontaneous passage."]$kp6$::jsonb,
      $pres6$Flank or abdominal pain (often vague/poorly localized in young children), gross or microscopic hematuria, dysuria/urinary frequency if the stone is near the bladder, nausea/vomiting, or an incidental finding on an ultrasound obtained for another indication. A concurrent UTI should raise suspicion for a struvite (infection) stone.$pres6$,
      $reas6$Confirm the stone with ultrasound (first-line) or non-contrast CT if ultrasound is equivocal and management hinges on the answer. Once confirmed, don't stop at symptomatic management — pursue the metabolic workup, since identifying and treating the underlying abnormality (most commonly hypercalciuria) is what prevents recurrence, and pediatric stone formers have a high recurrence rate if the cause isn't addressed.$reas6$,
      $tests6$Urinalysis (hematuria, pH, crystals), urine culture (rule out/identify infection stones), spot and 24-hour urine stone-risk panel (calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, cystine, sodium, creatinine, volume), serum electrolytes/calcium/phosphorus/PTH/uric acid, and stone composition analysis if a stone is passed or retrieved.$tests6$,
      $interp6$24-hour urine calcium >4 mg/kg/day (or an elevated age-adjusted spot Ca/Cr ratio) = hypercalciuria; low citrate = hypocitraturia (citrate normally inhibits calcium stone formation); elevated oxalate = hyperoxaluria (primary genetic or secondary to fat malabsorption/high oxalate intake); positive cyanide-nitroprusside test or elevated urine cystine = cystinuria; alkaline urine with a UTI from a urease-producing organism (e.g. Proteus) suggests a struvite stone.$interp6$,
      $img6$Renal/bladder ultrasound first-line (no radiation); non-contrast CT reserved for non-diagnostic ultrasound when the result would change management, given radiation concerns in children; a plain KUB can sometimes follow a known radio-opaque stone to track passage.$img6$,
      $tx6$Acute: pain control, hydration (IV if not tolerating PO), straining urine to catch a passed stone for analysis, and urology referral for stones causing obstruction, infection, intractable pain, or failure to pass. Chronic/preventive: increase fluid intake, moderate dietary sodium, and target the specific abnormality — thiazide diuretics for hypercalciuria (reduce urinary calcium excretion), potassium citrate for hypocitraturia (also alkalinizes urine, helpful for uric acid and cystine stones), allopurinol for hyperuricosuria, and cystine-binding agents (e.g. tiopronin, penicillamine) plus urine alkalinization for cystinuria.$tx6$,
      $rf6$A stone plus fever/signs of infection is a urologic emergency (obstructed, infected system) — needs urgent drainage (stent or nephrostomy), not just antibiotics alone. Don't skip the metabolic workup after "just one stone" — a first stone in a child is different from an adult and still warrants a full evaluation given the high prevalence of an identifiable, treatable cause.$rf6$,
      $pearl6$Board pearl: hypercalciuria is the most common metabolic abnormality found in pediatric stone formers — always calculate (or ask for) the age-adjusted urine Ca/Cr ratio. "Struvite/staghorn calculus" pairs with a urease-producing UTI organism (classically Proteus). Recurrent stones with a family history and a positive cyanide-nitroprusside urine test point to cystinuria.$pearl6$,
      $stem6$An 8-year-old girl presents with intermittent gross hematuria and mild flank pain. Renal ultrasound shows a 4 mm stone in the right kidney with mild hydronephrosis. She has a strong family history of kidney stones. A 24-hour urine collection shows calcium 4.8 mg/kg/day and normal oxalate and citrate.$stem6$,
      $cq6$["What metabolic abnormality does this 24-hour urine collection identify?","What is the first-line preventive therapy for this specific abnormality, and by what mechanism does it work?","What general lifestyle measure should be recommended regardless of the specific metabolic finding?"]$cq6$::jsonb,
      $disc6$A 24-hour urine calcium of 4.8 mg/kg/day (above the 4 mg/kg/day threshold) identifies hypercalciuria as the cause. First-line preventive therapy is a thiazide diuretic, which reduces urinary calcium excretion by increasing distal tubular calcium reabsorption. Regardless of the specific metabolic finding, increasing fluid intake to maintain a high urine volume (and diluting the concentration of stone-forming solutes) is recommended for essentially every stone former.$disc6$
    );
  else
    update public.academy_topics set
      category = 'Nephrolithiasis',
      summary = $sum6$Pediatric kidney stones are rising in incidence and, unlike in adults, up to 75% of children have an identifiable underlying metabolic abnormality — so every pediatric stone deserves a real metabolic workup, not just symptomatic management and reassurance.$sum6$,
      key_points = $kp6$["Risk factors: hypercalciuria (the most common metabolic abnormality found), hypocitraturia, hyperoxaluria, cystinuria, uric acid stones, urinary tract abnormalities/stasis (UPJ obstruction, VUR, neurogenic bladder), recurrent UTIs with urease-producing organisms (struvite stones), low fluid intake, and a family history of stones.","Presentation: classic renal colic (severe flank pain radiating to the groin) is less reliable in young children, who more often present with vague abdominal pain, gross or microscopic hematuria, or an incidental finding on imaging obtained for another reason.","First-line imaging is renal/bladder ultrasound (no radiation, good sensitivity for larger stones and hydronephrosis); non-contrast CT is more sensitive for small/ureteral stones but is reserved for cases where ultrasound is non-diagnostic and the diagnosis will change management, given radiation exposure in children.","Metabolic workup after a first stone: spot and/or 24-hour urine \"stone risk analysis\" — calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, cystine, sodium, creatinine — plus serum electrolytes, calcium, phosphorus, PTH, and uric acid; stone composition analysis if a stone is recovered.","Hypercalciuria workup uses the age-adjusted spot urine calcium/creatinine ratio (same cutoffs as in the Renal Function Tests topic: <7mo 0.86, 7-18mo 0.60, 19mo-6y 0.42, >6y 0.22 mg/mg) or a 24-hour urine calcium >4 mg/kg/day.","General prevention across almost all stone types: increased fluid intake (the single most broadly effective intervention), dietary sodium moderation (high sodium drives urinary calcium excretion), and targeted therapy for the specific abnormality found (thiazide for hypercalciuria, potassium citrate for hypocitraturia/to alkalinize urine, allopurinol for hyperuricosuria, cystine-binding agents for cystinuria).","Urologic intervention (lithotripsy, ureteroscopy, percutaneous nephrolithotomy) is reserved for stones causing obstruction, intractable pain, infection, or that fail to pass — most small distal stones are managed expectantly with hydration and pain control while awaiting spontaneous passage."]$kp6$::jsonb,
      presentation = $pres6$Flank or abdominal pain (often vague/poorly localized in young children), gross or microscopic hematuria, dysuria/urinary frequency if the stone is near the bladder, nausea/vomiting, or an incidental finding on an ultrasound obtained for another indication. A concurrent UTI should raise suspicion for a struvite (infection) stone.$pres6$,
      reasoning = $reas6$Confirm the stone with ultrasound (first-line) or non-contrast CT if ultrasound is equivocal and management hinges on the answer. Once confirmed, don't stop at symptomatic management — pursue the metabolic workup, since identifying and treating the underlying abnormality (most commonly hypercalciuria) is what prevents recurrence, and pediatric stone formers have a high recurrence rate if the cause isn't addressed.$reas6$,
      tests = $tests6$Urinalysis (hematuria, pH, crystals), urine culture (rule out/identify infection stones), spot and 24-hour urine stone-risk panel (calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, cystine, sodium, creatinine, volume), serum electrolytes/calcium/phosphorus/PTH/uric acid, and stone composition analysis if a stone is passed or retrieved.$tests6$,
      interpretation = $interp6$24-hour urine calcium >4 mg/kg/day (or an elevated age-adjusted spot Ca/Cr ratio) = hypercalciuria; low citrate = hypocitraturia (citrate normally inhibits calcium stone formation); elevated oxalate = hyperoxaluria (primary genetic or secondary to fat malabsorption/high oxalate intake); positive cyanide-nitroprusside test or elevated urine cystine = cystinuria; alkaline urine with a UTI from a urease-producing organism (e.g. Proteus) suggests a struvite stone.$interp6$,
      imaging = $img6$Renal/bladder ultrasound first-line (no radiation); non-contrast CT reserved for non-diagnostic ultrasound when the result would change management, given radiation concerns in children; a plain KUB can sometimes follow a known radio-opaque stone to track passage.$img6$,
      treatment = $tx6$Acute: pain control, hydration (IV if not tolerating PO), straining urine to catch a passed stone for analysis, and urology referral for stones causing obstruction, infection, intractable pain, or failure to pass. Chronic/preventive: increase fluid intake, moderate dietary sodium, and target the specific abnormality — thiazide diuretics for hypercalciuria (reduce urinary calcium excretion), potassium citrate for hypocitraturia (also alkalinizes urine, helpful for uric acid and cystine stones), allopurinol for hyperuricosuria, and cystine-binding agents (e.g. tiopronin, penicillamine) plus urine alkalinization for cystinuria.$tx6$,
      red_flags = $rf6$A stone plus fever/signs of infection is a urologic emergency (obstructed, infected system) — needs urgent drainage (stent or nephrostomy), not just antibiotics alone. Don't skip the metabolic workup after "just one stone" — a first stone in a child is different from an adult and still warrants a full evaluation given the high prevalence of an identifiable, treatable cause.$rf6$,
      pearls = $pearl6$Board pearl: hypercalciuria is the most common metabolic abnormality found in pediatric stone formers — always calculate (or ask for) the age-adjusted urine Ca/Cr ratio. "Struvite/staghorn calculus" pairs with a urease-producing UTI organism (classically Proteus). Recurrent stones with a family history and a positive cyanide-nitroprusside urine test point to cystinuria.$pearl6$,
      case_stem = $stem6$An 8-year-old girl presents with intermittent gross hematuria and mild flank pain. Renal ultrasound shows a 4 mm stone in the right kidney with mild hydronephrosis. She has a strong family history of kidney stones. A 24-hour urine collection shows calcium 4.8 mg/kg/day and normal oxalate and citrate.$stem6$,
      case_questions = $cq6$["What metabolic abnormality does this 24-hour urine collection identify?","What is the first-line preventive therapy for this specific abnormality, and by what mechanism does it work?","What general lifestyle measure should be recommended regardless of the specific metabolic finding?"]$cq6$::jsonb,
      case_discussion = $disc6$A 24-hour urine calcium of 4.8 mg/kg/day (above the 4 mg/kg/day threshold) identifies hypercalciuria as the cause. First-line preventive therapy is a thiazide diuretic, which reduces urinary calcium excretion by increasing distal tubular calcium reabsorption. Regardless of the specific metabolic finding, increasing fluid intake to maintain a high urine volume (and diluting the concentration of stone-forming solutes) is recommended for essentially every stone former.$disc6$
    where id = v_topic_id;
  end if;
end $$;

-- ============================================================
-- 7) Dialysis: Indications & Modalities
-- ============================================================
do $$
declare
  v_owner_id uuid;
  v_parent_id uuid;
  v_topic_id uuid;
begin
  select id into v_owner_id from auth.users where email = 'alihessamis@gmail.com' limit 1;
  if v_owner_id is null then
    raise exception 'owner not found for email alihessamis@gmail.com';
  end if;

  select id into v_parent_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and category ilike '%Dialysis%' and parent_topic_id is null
    limit 1;

  select id into v_topic_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%Dialysis%Indications%'
    limit 1;

  if v_topic_id is null then
    insert into public.academy_topics (
      owner_id, category, parent_topic_id, name, summary, key_points, presentation, reasoning, tests,
      interpretation, imaging, treatment, red_flags, pearls, case_stem, case_questions, case_discussion
    ) values (
      v_owner_id, 'Dialysis', v_parent_id, 'Dialysis: Indications & Modalities',
      $sum7$Knowing exactly when a child needs urgent dialysis — and which modality fits the clinical situation — is one of the highest-yield acute-nephrology topics on the boards: the classic indications (the "AEIOU"-style acronym) and the practical differences between PD, HD, and continuous therapies come up constantly.$sum7$,
      $kp7$["Indications for acute dialysis (classic AEIOU-style list): refractory metabolic Acidosis, Electrolyte abnormality (especially severe/refractory hyperkalemia >6.5-7 mEq/L with ECG changes), Ingestion of a dialyzable toxin/drug, refractory volume Overload (especially with pulmonary edema/respiratory compromise), and Uremia (symptomatic, or BUN roughly >150 mg/dL) — any one of these, refractory to medical management, is an indication.","Peritoneal dialysis (PD): uses the peritoneal membrane as the filter via a surgically placed catheter; advantages are technical simplicity, no need for vascular access or anticoagulation, and gentler/more gradual fluid and solute removal (better tolerated hemodynamically); contraindicated with a recent abdominal surgery, peritonitis, abdominal wall defects, or a ventriculoperitoneal shunt in some cases.","Hemodialysis (HD): requires vascular access (central venous catheter acutely, or an AV fistula/graft for chronic use) and anticoagulation; removes solutes and fluid much faster than PD, useful when rapid correction is needed (severe hyperkalemia, toxin removal), but carries more hemodynamic instability risk, especially in small/unstable children.","Continuous renal replacement therapy (CVVH/CVVHD, sometimes still called CAVH): a continuous, slower extracorporeal technique typically used in the ICU for hemodynamically unstable children who cannot tolerate the rapid fluid/solute shifts of intermittent HD; requires central venous access and anticoagulation, and allows precise, gradual fluid removal over 24 hours.","PD is generally preferred as first-line acute dialysis in small infants and hemodynamically unstable children when it isn't contraindicated, because of its gentler physiology and lack of need for vascular access.","Complications differ by modality: PD — peritonitis (most common), catheter malfunction/leak, hernia, protein loss; HD — vascular access complications (thrombosis, infection), disequilibrium syndrome (cerebral edema from rapid osmotic shifts, especially with a very high starting BUN corrected too fast), hypotension; CRRT — line-related complications, anticoagulation-related bleeding, hypothermia."]$kp7$::jsonb,
      $pres7$The decision point is usually a child with AKI or decompensated CKD who has one of the AEIOU indications despite maximal medical therapy: refractory hyperkalemia on ECG monitoring, worsening pulmonary edema despite diuretics, a dangerous toxin ingestion, or an uremic child with altered mental status/pericarditis/bleeding.$pres7$,
      $reas7$First ask "does this child need dialysis at all" using the AEIOU framework, then "which modality" based on hemodynamic stability, need for speed (toxin removal favors HD/CRRT), available access, and any contraindications to PD (recent abdominal surgery, VP shunt, peritonitis). In practice, unstable small children often start on PD or CRRT; a stable older child needing rapid correction often goes to intermittent HD.$reas7$,
      $tests7$Electrolytes and blood gas (to confirm and trend the acidosis/hyperkalemia driving the decision), BUN/creatinine, and a toxicology screen/level when a dialyzable ingestion is suspected; ongoing labs during dialysis to track correction and avoid overly rapid shifts.$tests7$,
      $interp7$Any AEIOU criterion that is refractory to medical management (e.g. hyperkalemia not responding to calcium/insulin-glucose/bicarbonate, or volume overload not responding to diuretics) is an indication to proceed to dialysis rather than continue escalating medical therapy alone.$interp7$,
      $img7$Not primarily imaging-driven, though a chest X-ray is often obtained to confirm and follow pulmonary edema when volume overload is the indication, and PD catheter placement/position can be confirmed radiographically.$img7$,
      $tx7$Choose the modality per the reasoning above, correct electrolyte/acid-base abnormalities gradually to avoid disequilibrium syndrome (especially with a very elevated starting BUN), and treat the underlying disease in parallel — dialysis is a bridge/support, not a cure for the primary kidney disease.$tx7$,
      $rf7$Correcting a very high BUN/severe azotemia too rapidly with aggressive HD risks dialysis disequilibrium syndrome (cerebral edema, seizures) — start with a gentler initial session (shorter duration/lower blood flow) in a markedly uremic patient. Missing a refractory-hyperkalemia-with-ECG-changes indication while continuing to "try one more medical therapy" can be fatal.$rf7$,
      $pearl7$Board pearl: "AEIOU" (Acidosis, Electrolytes, Ingestion, Overload, Uremia) is the fastest way to scan a vignette for a dialysis indication. PD is the classic first-choice acute modality in a small, hemodynamically unstable infant without a contraindication; CRRT is the answer when the vignette describes an unstable ICU patient who can't tolerate intermittent HD's rapid shifts.$pearl7$,
      $stem7$A 6-year-old with AKI from severe dehydration has a potassium of 7.4 mEq/L with peaked T waves on ECG. He has received IV calcium gluconate, insulin/glucose, and albuterol, but repeat potassium is 7.1 mEq/L 30 minutes later. He is hemodynamically stable.$stem7$,
      $cq7$["Which AEIOU indication does this case meet, and why is it appropriate to proceed to dialysis now?","Given that he is stable with no contraindications, which modality would typically be favored, and why?","What precaution should be taken given how markedly abnormal his labs are, to avoid a dialysis-specific complication?"]$cq7$::jsonb,
      $disc7$This meets the "Electrolyte abnormality" (E) indication — refractory, ECG-changing hyperkalemia that hasn't responded to medical therapy is an indication for urgent dialysis regardless of hemodynamic stability. In a stable child without contraindications, PD or HD are both reasonable, but if rapid potassium correction is the priority, HD removes solute fastest; if access or stability favors a gentler approach, PD is also effective though slower. Because starting values can be markedly deranged, care should be taken to avoid overly rapid correction (though this is more classically discussed with severe azotemia/BUN than potassium) — in general, gradual correction on the first session reduces the risk of disequilibrium-type complications.$disc7$
    );
  else
    update public.academy_topics set
      category = 'Dialysis',
      summary = $sum7$Knowing exactly when a child needs urgent dialysis — and which modality fits the clinical situation — is one of the highest-yield acute-nephrology topics on the boards: the classic indications (the "AEIOU"-style acronym) and the practical differences between PD, HD, and continuous therapies come up constantly.$sum7$,
      key_points = $kp7$["Indications for acute dialysis (classic AEIOU-style list): refractory metabolic Acidosis, Electrolyte abnormality (especially severe/refractory hyperkalemia >6.5-7 mEq/L with ECG changes), Ingestion of a dialyzable toxin/drug, refractory volume Overload (especially with pulmonary edema/respiratory compromise), and Uremia (symptomatic, or BUN roughly >150 mg/dL) — any one of these, refractory to medical management, is an indication.","Peritoneal dialysis (PD): uses the peritoneal membrane as the filter via a surgically placed catheter; advantages are technical simplicity, no need for vascular access or anticoagulation, and gentler/more gradual fluid and solute removal (better tolerated hemodynamically); contraindicated with a recent abdominal surgery, peritonitis, abdominal wall defects, or a ventriculoperitoneal shunt in some cases.","Hemodialysis (HD): requires vascular access (central venous catheter acutely, or an AV fistula/graft for chronic use) and anticoagulation; removes solutes and fluid much faster than PD, useful when rapid correction is needed (severe hyperkalemia, toxin removal), but carries more hemodynamic instability risk, especially in small/unstable children.","Continuous renal replacement therapy (CVVH/CVVHD, sometimes still called CAVH): a continuous, slower extracorporeal technique typically used in the ICU for hemodynamically unstable children who cannot tolerate the rapid fluid/solute shifts of intermittent HD; requires central venous access and anticoagulation, and allows precise, gradual fluid removal over 24 hours.","PD is generally preferred as first-line acute dialysis in small infants and hemodynamically unstable children when it isn't contraindicated, because of its gentler physiology and lack of need for vascular access.","Complications differ by modality: PD — peritonitis (most common), catheter malfunction/leak, hernia, protein loss; HD — vascular access complications (thrombosis, infection), disequilibrium syndrome (cerebral edema from rapid osmotic shifts, especially with a very high starting BUN corrected too fast), hypotension; CRRT — line-related complications, anticoagulation-related bleeding, hypothermia."]$kp7$::jsonb,
      presentation = $pres7$The decision point is usually a child with AKI or decompensated CKD who has one of the AEIOU indications despite maximal medical therapy: refractory hyperkalemia on ECG monitoring, worsening pulmonary edema despite diuretics, a dangerous toxin ingestion, or an uremic child with altered mental status/pericarditis/bleeding.$pres7$,
      reasoning = $reas7$First ask "does this child need dialysis at all" using the AEIOU framework, then "which modality" based on hemodynamic stability, need for speed (toxin removal favors HD/CRRT), available access, and any contraindications to PD (recent abdominal surgery, VP shunt, peritonitis). In practice, unstable small children often start on PD or CRRT; a stable older child needing rapid correction often goes to intermittent HD.$reas7$,
      tests = $tests7$Electrolytes and blood gas (to confirm and trend the acidosis/hyperkalemia driving the decision), BUN/creatinine, and a toxicology screen/level when a dialyzable ingestion is suspected; ongoing labs during dialysis to track correction and avoid overly rapid shifts.$tests7$,
      interpretation = $interp7$Any AEIOU criterion that is refractory to medical management (e.g. hyperkalemia not responding to calcium/insulin-glucose/bicarbonate, or volume overload not responding to diuretics) is an indication to proceed to dialysis rather than continue escalating medical therapy alone.$interp7$,
      imaging = $img7$Not primarily imaging-driven, though a chest X-ray is often obtained to confirm and follow pulmonary edema when volume overload is the indication, and PD catheter placement/position can be confirmed radiographically.$img7$,
      treatment = $tx7$Choose the modality per the reasoning above, correct electrolyte/acid-base abnormalities gradually to avoid disequilibrium syndrome (especially with a very elevated starting BUN), and treat the underlying disease in parallel — dialysis is a bridge/support, not a cure for the primary kidney disease.$tx7$,
      red_flags = $rf7$Correcting a very high BUN/severe azotemia too rapidly with aggressive HD risks dialysis disequilibrium syndrome (cerebral edema, seizures) — start with a gentler initial session (shorter duration/lower blood flow) in a markedly uremic patient. Missing a refractory-hyperkalemia-with-ECG-changes indication while continuing to "try one more medical therapy" can be fatal.$rf7$,
      pearls = $pearl7$Board pearl: "AEIOU" (Acidosis, Electrolytes, Ingestion, Overload, Uremia) is the fastest way to scan a vignette for a dialysis indication. PD is the classic first-choice acute modality in a small, hemodynamically unstable infant without a contraindication; CRRT is the answer when the vignette describes an unstable ICU patient who can't tolerate intermittent HD's rapid shifts.$pearl7$,
      case_stem = $stem7$A 6-year-old with AKI from severe dehydration has a potassium of 7.4 mEq/L with peaked T waves on ECG. He has received IV calcium gluconate, insulin/glucose, and albuterol, but repeat potassium is 7.1 mEq/L 30 minutes later. He is hemodynamically stable.$stem7$,
      case_questions = $cq7$["Which AEIOU indication does this case meet, and why is it appropriate to proceed to dialysis now?","Given that he is stable with no contraindications, which modality would typically be favored, and why?","What precaution should be taken given how markedly abnormal his labs are, to avoid a dialysis-specific complication?"]$cq7$::jsonb,
      case_discussion = $disc7$This meets the "Electrolyte abnormality" (E) indication — refractory, ECG-changing hyperkalemia that hasn't responded to medical therapy is an indication for urgent dialysis regardless of hemodynamic stability. In a stable child without contraindications, PD or HD are both reasonable, but if rapid potassium correction is the priority, HD removes solute fastest; if access or stability favors a gentler approach, PD is also effective though slower. Because starting values can be markedly deranged, care should be taken to avoid overly rapid correction (though this is more classically discussed with severe azotemia/BUN than potassium) — in general, gradual correction on the first session reduces the risk of disequilibrium-type complications.$disc7$
    where id = v_topic_id;
  end if;
end $$;

-- ============================================================
-- 8) Hypertensive Crisis
-- (from Harriet Lane Handbook Ch.1 "Emergency Management", section III.E —
-- the one nephrology-relevant piece of that otherwise general-emergency-
-- medicine chapter; drug names/doses are given as general reference —
-- always confirm exact per-kg dosing against your institution's current
-- protocol/formulary before administering.)
-- ============================================================
do $$
declare
  v_owner_id uuid;
  v_parent_id uuid;
  v_topic_id uuid;
begin
  select id into v_owner_id from auth.users where email = 'alihessamis@gmail.com' limit 1;
  if v_owner_id is null then
    raise exception 'owner not found for email alihessamis@gmail.com';
  end if;

  select id into v_parent_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and category ilike '%Hypertension%' and parent_topic_id is null
    limit 1;

  select id into v_topic_id from public.academy_topics
    where owner_id = v_owner_id and name ilike '%Hypertensive Crisis%'
    limit 1;

  if v_topic_id is null then
    insert into public.academy_topics (
      owner_id, category, parent_topic_id, name, summary, key_points, presentation, reasoning, tests,
      interpretation, imaging, treatment, red_flags, pearls, case_stem, case_questions, case_discussion
    ) values (
      v_owner_id, 'Hypertension', v_parent_id, 'Hypertensive Crisis',
      $sum8$A hypertensive crisis is a severe BP elevation (well above the 95th percentile for age/sex/height, per the AAP 2017 tables already used elsewhere in this app) that is split into emergency (with acute end-organ damage) vs. urgency (without) — a distinction that determines how fast and by what route you have to act.$sum8$,
      $kp8$["Hypertensive emergency = severe hypertension WITH evidence of acute end-organ damage: hypertensive encephalopathy (headache, altered mental status, seizure), papilledema/visual changes, pulmonary edema/heart failure, or acute kidney injury — this requires immediate IV therapy and ICU-level monitoring.","Hypertensive urgency = severe hypertension WITHOUT acute end-organ damage — still needs prompt treatment and close monitoring, but generally allows oral agents and a somewhat less emergent (though not casual) timeline.","Normal BP values for age/sex/height are defined in the companion Growth & BP chart in this app (AAP 2017 tables), the same reference this chapter itself points back to — there is no separate infant (<1 year) percentile table in either Harriet Lane chapter reviewed for this app.","The cardinal safety principle in treating a hypertensive emergency: reduce BP gradually, not to normal immediately — an overly rapid drop can cause cerebral hypoperfusion/ischemia in a brain that has autoregulated to a higher pressure; a common target is roughly a 25% reduction in the first 8 hours, with gradual normalization over the following 24-48 hours.","Continuous-infusion IV agents used for hypertensive emergencies (titratable, allowing tight control) include nicardipine (a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker, popular first-line choice in many pediatric centers for its smooth titratability), labetalol (combined alpha/beta-blocker), and sodium nitroprusside (potent arterial+venous vasodilator, very rapid onset/offset, but carries a risk of cyanide/thiocyanate toxicity with prolonged use or in renal impairment, requiring monitoring).","Bolus/intermittent IV agents include hydralazine (direct arteriolar vasodilator, can cause reflex tachycardia) and labetalol boluses; oral agents for urgency or step-down therapy include a short-acting calcium-channel blocker, an ACE inhibitor, or clonidine — clonidine should never be stopped abruptly once started, since it can cause rebound hypertension.","Exact per-kg/per-dose numbers vary by agent, indication, and local protocol — confirm current dosing against your institution's formulary or a dosing reference (e.g. Lexicomp) at the time of use rather than relying on memorized figures."]$kp8$::jsonb,
      $pres8$Headache, vomiting, visual changes, altered mental status, or a seizure (hypertensive encephalopathy); respiratory distress from pulmonary edema; or an incidentally severely elevated BP reading found during evaluation for another complaint. In a child with known CKD, dialysis, or a renal transplant, a severe BP elevation should always be taken seriously even if the child looks otherwise well, since baseline autoregulation may already be shifted.$pres8$,
      $reas8$First classify emergency vs. urgency by looking for end-organ damage (mental status, fundoscopic exam for papilledema, cardiopulmonary exam for pulmonary edema, and a basic metabolic panel/creatinine for AKI). That classification, not the absolute BP number alone, determines route (IV continuous infusion vs. oral) and monitoring intensity (ICU vs. close observation).$reas8$,
      $tests8$Repeat BP measurement to confirm (correct cuff size, calm/rested child), fundoscopic exam, basic metabolic panel and creatinine, urinalysis, and — guided by the presentation — head imaging if encephalopathy/seizure, chest X-ray/echocardiogram if heart failure is suspected, and a workup for the underlying cause of hypertension (renal, renovascular, endocrine) once acutely stabilized.$tests8$,
      $interp8$Any of encephalopathy, papilledema, pulmonary edema, or AKI accompanying severe hypertension = emergency, treat with IV agents and admit to a monitored/ICU setting. Severe hypertension without any of these = urgency, oral therapy and close monitoring/rapid follow-up are usually appropriate.$interp8$,
      $img8$Not the first step, but guided by the clinical picture: head CT/MRI if encephalopathy or a focal neurologic finding is present, chest X-ray if pulmonary edema is suspected, and — once stabilized — renal ultrasound with Doppler as part of the workup for an underlying renal or renovascular cause.$img8$,
      $tx8$Hypertensive emergency: continuous IV infusion (e.g. nicardipine or labetalol; sodium nitroprusside for very rapid, tightly monitored control) in an ICU/monitored setting, targeting a gradual reduction (roughly 25% in the first 8 hours) rather than rapid normalization, with frequent BP checks and titration. Hypertensive urgency: oral agents (e.g. a short-acting calcium-channel blocker or clonidine) with close follow-up; IV therapy is reserved for emergencies or when oral therapy fails/isn't tolerated. In either case, treat and search for the underlying cause once the acute crisis is controlled (renal parenchymal disease is the most common pediatric cause overall, renovascular disease and endocrine causes are more common in specific age/presentation patterns).$tx8$,
      $rf8$Do not attempt to normalize blood pressure rapidly/all at once in a hypertensive emergency — this is the classic error that can precipitate cerebral ischemia. Never abruptly stop clonidine once a patient is established on it, due to rebound hypertension. A child with known CKD/ESRD/transplant and a new severe BP elevation should be evaluated for a specific reversible cause (medication nonadherence, rejection, renal artery stenosis) rather than simply escalating antihypertensive doses.$rf8$,
      $pearl8$Board pearl: "severe hypertension + seizure/altered mental status/papilledema/pulmonary edema/AKI" = hypertensive emergency -> IV continuous infusion, ICU, gradual (not rapid) correction. "Severe hypertension, well-appearing, no end-organ findings" = urgency -> oral agent, close follow-up. Nicardipine's easy titratability is why it's frequently favored as a first continuous-infusion agent in pediatric hypertensive emergencies.$pearl8$,
      $stem8$A 10-year-old boy with known chronic glomerulonephritis presents with severe headache, one witnessed seizure, and a blood pressure markedly above the 99th percentile for his age/height. Fundoscopic exam shows papilledema.$stem8$,
      $cq8$["Is this a hypertensive emergency or urgency, and why?","What is the appropriate initial route and setting for treatment?","What is the key safety principle to follow when lowering this child's blood pressure, and why?"]$cq8$::jsonb,
      $disc8$This is a hypertensive emergency — severe hypertension with acute end-organ damage (seizure, papilledema, both suggesting hypertensive encephalopathy). He needs IV continuous-infusion antihypertensive therapy (e.g. nicardipine or labetalol) in an ICU/monitored setting, not an oral agent. The key safety principle is gradual BP reduction — roughly 25% in the first 8 hours with further normalization over 24-48 hours — because his cerebral autoregulation has adapted to a higher baseline pressure, and dropping the BP too quickly risks cerebral hypoperfusion/ischemia rather than helping him.$disc8$
    );
  else
    update public.academy_topics set
      category = 'Hypertension',
      summary = $sum8$A hypertensive crisis is a severe BP elevation (well above the 95th percentile for age/sex/height, per the AAP 2017 tables already used elsewhere in this app) that is split into emergency (with acute end-organ damage) vs. urgency (without) — a distinction that determines how fast and by what route you have to act.$sum8$,
      key_points = $kp8$["Hypertensive emergency = severe hypertension WITH evidence of acute end-organ damage: hypertensive encephalopathy (headache, altered mental status, seizure), papilledema/visual changes, pulmonary edema/heart failure, or acute kidney injury — this requires immediate IV therapy and ICU-level monitoring.","Hypertensive urgency = severe hypertension WITHOUT acute end-organ damage — still needs prompt treatment and close monitoring, but generally allows oral agents and a somewhat less emergent (though not casual) timeline.","Normal BP values for age/sex/height are defined in the companion Growth & BP chart in this app (AAP 2017 tables), the same reference this chapter itself points back to — there is no separate infant (<1 year) percentile table in either Harriet Lane chapter reviewed for this app.","The cardinal safety principle in treating a hypertensive emergency: reduce BP gradually, not to normal immediately — an overly rapid drop can cause cerebral hypoperfusion/ischemia in a brain that has autoregulated to a higher pressure; a common target is roughly a 25% reduction in the first 8 hours, with gradual normalization over the following 24-48 hours.","Continuous-infusion IV agents used for hypertensive emergencies (titratable, allowing tight control) include nicardipine (a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker, popular first-line choice in many pediatric centers for its smooth titratability), labetalol (combined alpha/beta-blocker), and sodium nitroprusside (potent arterial+venous vasodilator, very rapid onset/offset, but carries a risk of cyanide/thiocyanate toxicity with prolonged use or in renal impairment, requiring monitoring).","Bolus/intermittent IV agents include hydralazine (direct arteriolar vasodilator, can cause reflex tachycardia) and labetalol boluses; oral agents for urgency or step-down therapy include a short-acting calcium-channel blocker, an ACE inhibitor, or clonidine — clonidine should never be stopped abruptly once started, since it can cause rebound hypertension.","Exact per-kg/per-dose numbers vary by agent, indication, and local protocol — confirm current dosing against your institution's formulary or a dosing reference (e.g. Lexicomp) at the time of use rather than relying on memorized figures."]$kp8$::jsonb,
      presentation = $pres8$Headache, vomiting, visual changes, altered mental status, or a seizure (hypertensive encephalopathy); respiratory distress from pulmonary edema; or an incidentally severely elevated BP reading found during evaluation for another complaint. In a child with known CKD, dialysis, or a renal transplant, a severe BP elevation should always be taken seriously even if the child looks otherwise well, since baseline autoregulation may already be shifted.$pres8$,
      reasoning = $reas8$First classify emergency vs. urgency by looking for end-organ damage (mental status, fundoscopic exam for papilledema, cardiopulmonary exam for pulmonary edema, and a basic metabolic panel/creatinine for AKI). That classification, not the absolute BP number alone, determines route (IV continuous infusion vs. oral) and monitoring intensity (ICU vs. close observation).$reas8$,
      tests = $tests8$Repeat BP measurement to confirm (correct cuff size, calm/rested child), fundoscopic exam, basic metabolic panel and creatinine, urinalysis, and — guided by the presentation — head imaging if encephalopathy/seizure, chest X-ray/echocardiogram if heart failure is suspected, and a workup for the underlying cause of hypertension (renal, renovascular, endocrine) once acutely stabilized.$tests8$,
      interpretation = $interp8$Any of encephalopathy, papilledema, pulmonary edema, or AKI accompanying severe hypertension = emergency, treat with IV agents and admit to a monitored/ICU setting. Severe hypertension without any of these = urgency, oral therapy and close monitoring/rapid follow-up are usually appropriate.$interp8$,
      imaging = $img8$Not the first step, but guided by the clinical picture: head CT/MRI if encephalopathy or a focal neurologic finding is present, chest X-ray if pulmonary edema is suspected, and — once stabilized — renal ultrasound with Doppler as part of the workup for an underlying renal or renovascular cause.$img8$,
      treatment = $tx8$Hypertensive emergency: continuous IV infusion (e.g. nicardipine or labetalol; sodium nitroprusside for very rapid, tightly monitored control) in an ICU/monitored setting, targeting a gradual reduction (roughly 25% in the first 8 hours) rather than rapid normalization, with frequent BP checks and titration. Hypertensive urgency: oral agents (e.g. a short-acting calcium-channel blocker or clonidine) with close follow-up; IV therapy is reserved for emergencies or when oral therapy fails/isn't tolerated. In either case, treat and search for the underlying cause once the acute crisis is controlled (renal parenchymal disease is the most common pediatric cause overall, renovascular disease and endocrine causes are more common in specific age/presentation patterns).$tx8$,
      red_flags = $rf8$Do not attempt to normalize blood pressure rapidly/all at once in a hypertensive emergency — this is the classic error that can precipitate cerebral ischemia. Never abruptly stop clonidine once a patient is established on it, due to rebound hypertension. A child with known CKD/ESRD/transplant and a new severe BP elevation should be evaluated for a specific reversible cause (medication nonadherence, rejection, renal artery stenosis) rather than simply escalating antihypertensive doses.$rf8$,
      pearls = $pearl8$Board pearl: "severe hypertension + seizure/altered mental status/papilledema/pulmonary edema/AKI" = hypertensive emergency -> IV continuous infusion, ICU, gradual (not rapid) correction. "Severe hypertension, well-appearing, no end-organ findings" = urgency -> oral agent, close follow-up. Nicardipine's easy titratability is why it's frequently favored as a first continuous-infusion agent in pediatric hypertensive emergencies.$pearl8$,
      case_stem = $stem8$A 10-year-old boy with known chronic glomerulonephritis presents with severe headache, one witnessed seizure, and a blood pressure markedly above the 99th percentile for his age/height. Fundoscopic exam shows papilledema.$stem8$,
      case_questions = $cq8$["Is this a hypertensive emergency or urgency, and why?","What is the appropriate initial route and setting for treatment?","What is the key safety principle to follow when lowering this child's blood pressure, and why?"]$cq8$::jsonb,
      case_discussion = $disc8$This is a hypertensive emergency — severe hypertension with acute end-organ damage (seizure, papilledema, both suggesting hypertensive encephalopathy). He needs IV continuous-infusion antihypertensive therapy (e.g. nicardipine or labetalol) in an ICU/monitored setting, not an oral agent. The key safety principle is gradual BP reduction — roughly 25% in the first 8 hours with further normalization over 24-48 hours — because his cerebral autoregulation has adapted to a higher baseline pressure, and dropping the BP too quickly risks cerebral hypoperfusion/ischemia rather than helping him.$disc8$
    where id = v_topic_id;
  end if;
end $$;
